\par\par \b \uc2  Fitch: Strong Yen May Challenge Japan's Less Dim Outlook \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 943 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 18:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Despite the prevailing gloomy backdrop to Japan's long stagnant economy, credit ratings agency Fitch says it is seeing "a turning point" amid some signs of incipient improvement in economic data over the past six months, even though the agency continues to warn of persistent structural defects weighing on the economy over the longer term. \par \par \uc2  "In general, the consensus now is that the outlook in Japan is better than six months ago," said Brian Coulton, a senior director at Fitch's Asia-Pacific sovereign ratings group, in an exclusive interview with Dow Jones Newswires. Coulton, who is based in London, was speaking during a visit to New York. He cited some "surprisingly good numbers" including recent industrial production and stronger-than-expected first quarter consumption data out of Japan. \par \par \uc2  For now, the main obstacle that could derail an export-led recovery in Japan before it gathers momentum is the strengthening of the yen during recent weeks, the credit rating agency said. "A stronger yen is the last thing Japan needs right now," Coulton said. \par \par \uc2  Coulton's comments mark the first official recognition from a ratings agency that the cyclical recovery in Japan is sparking a shift in market sentiment, which in turn has helped propel the yen almost 6% higher over the past month. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Wednesday's global trading session, Japanese authorities finally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest the sharp slide of the dollar against the yen, stepping into the market at levels around Y123.70, in a reflection of the government's concerns that the rallying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could snuff out the nascent cyclical recovery in Japan. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists have consistently identified a broad area between Y125 and Y120 to the dollar where the Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although the firming of the yen may be a near term phenomenon, partly driven by speculative trades to test out how much ground the dollar may give against the yen, Coulton of Fitch and some other analysts at banks and U.S.-based investment funds also cite some longer term portfolio flows driving the rally. That's based on a shift of foreign investors' perceptions toward a less dismal view of Japan, they said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Turning Point\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is an accumulation of evidence that a turning point (in the\par Japanese economy) has been reached," Coulton said, even though he noted\par that Fitch's current outlook on Japan's sovereign rating remains\par negative, which is unlikely to change over approximately a one-year\par horizon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Supporting more positive sentiment on Japanese assets, several U.S.\par securities firms have issued recommendations to overweight Japanese\par equities during recent weeks, which have caused discernible shifts in\par portfolio flows, with longer-term funds moving into yen-denominated\par assets, said Carl Weinberg chief economist at High Frequency Economics\par in Valhalla, N.Y.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Furthermore, the Japanese cabinet's statement last week that the\par domestic economy was bottoming out has fueled more of a bid for the yen.\par Although Weinberg argues some analysts expectations of anything more\par than feeble growth are way too optimistic, "the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets have\par bought into this story substantially," Weinberg said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indeed, Coulton noted that for Japan's economy, it remains doubtful\par whether the "short-term improvement will turn into sustained recovery."\par Despite the government's reduction of the fiscal deficit in fiscal year\par 2001 - and Japan's large current account surpluses - the longer-term\par outlook will likely deteriorate, probably causing the yen eventually to\par weaken once more, Fitch said. The country faces "a slow burn\par deterioration," Coulton said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Two factors that continue to plague Japan's longer- term prospects\par remain the huge mountain of bad and non-performing loans in the banking\par system, a bill that credit ratings agencies expect the government to\par eventually have to at least partially foot, causing public debt to\par balloon further. Fitch expects Japan's ratio of debt-to-gross domestic\par product, currently around 140%, to approach 150% by the end of March\par 2003.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, credit rating agency Moody's Investors Service has\par consistently stated that the embedded losses in the banking system\par amount to between 10% and 15% of gross domestic product. For its part,\par Fitch estimated Tuesday that these losses are around 9.5% of GDP.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    All Eyes On Moody's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese government will have to undertake some recapitalization\par of the major banks, conditional on banks improving their asset\par management and lending policies, Coulton said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Any such recapitalization would likely involve the government stepping\par up debt issuance, which could weigh heavily on prices of Japanese\par Government Bonds, or JGBs, global sovereign debt strategists warn.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late last year Fitch cut its Japan's rating to the current "AA", two\par notches off the top ratings, maintaining a negative outlook. Standard &\par Poor's cut its Japan rating to double-A minus with a negative outlook on\par April 15.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But among the big three credit rating agencies, capital markets\par participants are most intensely focused on Moody's, which has delayed a\par decision whether to downgrade Japan by as much as two notches originally\par scheduled by May 13. The agency is now aiming to make that decision by\par May 31. Moody's signaled routine reasons for the delay, including the\par need for more time to complete preparations for the decision.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moody's downgraded Japan's long-term debt rating one notch to "Aa3" in\par December, citing increasing fiscal strains, economic weakness,\par ineffective reform initiatives and the absence of quick-fix policy\par remedies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020522dy5m007pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
