\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Safe gold sets new high on global jitters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alden Bentley \par \uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 21:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 22 (Reuters) - COMEX gold set a new high for the year Wednesday near $320 an ounce on market nervousness over hair-trigger tensions between India and Pakistan and the threat of another terror attack on New York landmarks. \par \par \uc2  Gold has morphed from a dog into the darling of the investment world in 2002. The groundwork was laid in last year's U.S. recession, when 11 interest rate cuts made low gold yields look better against dollar deposits and investors looking for safe havens after the Sept 11 terror attacks on American soil helped gold turn the corner on a 20-year bear market. \par \par \uc2  In its sixth straight gain, benchmark June gold ended up $2.20 at $318.30 an ounce, moving from $315.60 to $319.80, its highest since June 2000. Estimated volume was a busy 42,000 contracts, including rollovers. \par \par \uc2  "It's a nice combination for gold at the moment - the Israeli-Palestinian thing which is smoldering but still a factor; the India-Pakistan thing which is very hot; the equity market is still somewhat soft; the dollar is getting beaten up," said James Pogoda, a vice president of precious metals at Mitsubishi International Corp. \par \par \uc2  Gold prices are up 14 percent since Jan 2. Mining shares leveraged that into a 60 percent rise this year, based on the XAU Index of gold and silver equities at the Philadelphia Stock Exchange, which rose to a 2-1/2 year high at 87.00 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold closed at $318.10/60, up from Tuesday's close at $315.80/6.30 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In London, it fixed late Wednesday at $317.10 an ounce, and topped at $319.60, its highest since the massive spike to $338 in October 1999 on the back of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement to limit sales and dispel the worry that pushed bullion to a 20-year low in August of that year. \par \par \uc2  Gold's traditional flight-to-quality allure was enhanced on Wednesday after India's Prime Minister Atal Behari Vajpayee told Indian soldiers confronting Pakistani forces across their border that the time had come for a decisive fight. \par \par \uc2  The neighbors, both nuclear powers, have amassed more than a million troops along the border since India blamed a December attack on its parliament on Pakistan-backed Islamic militants. \par \par \uc2  The armies have exchanged heavy fire for six days running after another deadly assault last week on an Indian army camp by suspected Pakistan-based Kashmiri separatists left 30 dead, mostly women and children. \par \par \uc2  London gold traders were spooked by news that Britain was set to withdraw some of its embassy staff from Pakistan in response to security threats there. \par \par \uc2  "People are definitely jittery and taking a wait-and-see attitude," said a New York bullion trader. "At this level I don't think anyone is aggressive to initiate positions. Possibly all the short-covering has been done and longs are hanging in there." \par \par \uc2  In New York, the temporary closure of the Brooklyn Bridge after the discovery of a suspicious package, later found not to be a bomb, also unnerved traders. \par \par \uc2  Federal law enforcement officials had also reported vague threats against the Statue of Liberty and the city imposed the toughest security measures since just after the World Trade Center was destroyed by suicide hijackers 8-1/2 months ago. \par \par \uc2  A major factor in the rise of gold, which was historically seen as a form of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is the recent dollar fall, which makes bullion cheaper for investors outside the United States. \par \par \uc2  The greenback hit a 28-month low Wednesday against the Swiss franc, another popular safe-haven. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen, which rose to a five-month high against the dollar, while the euro rose to an eight-month high. \par \par \uc2  "The gold rally is continuing apparently unabated as a function of a weaker dollar and the increased political risk globally," said James Steel, analyst at Refco Inc. "Continued good physical demand is reported in Japan and various parts of Asia centering on their own financial concerns." \par \par \uc2  July silver slipped half a cent to $4.84 an ounce, digesting Tuesday's surge to a 15-month high at $4.865. Wednesday's range was $4.86 to $4.78. Spot silver closed unchanged at $4.82/84 and fixed at $4.82. \par \par \uc2  NYMEX July platinum rose $2 to $540 an ounce. Spot platinum was quoted late at $542/549. \par \par \uc2  Thinly traded June palladium fell $9.15 to $361 an ounce, continuing a $12.65 shakeout on Tuesday. Spot palladium fetched $354/369. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020522dy5m00x6j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
