\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ upgrades view, but recovery doubts linger. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yonggi Kang and Jason Szep \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 16:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 22 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan upgraded its view of the economy for a third straight month on Wednesday as a key barometer of service industries - from sushi restaurants to auto repair shops - rose for the first time in four months. \par \par \uc2  The upbeat news suggests an export-driven flurry of first-quarter manufacturing activity was spreading to other sectors, taking pressure off the government to stimulate growth as Japan pulls out of its longest downturn in 50 years. \par \par \uc2  A surprisingly strong 1.2 percent gain in the government's tertiary industries index in March - the biggest month-on-month rise in a year - showed service companies in the transport, telecoms and retail sectors starting to recover. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its monthly report also cited some risks, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The yen has climbed to five-month highs against the dollar, a worry for exporters who reaped profit windfalls from a big drop in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year. \par \par \uc2  "The pace of deterioration in Japan's economy has moderated, with production starting to pick up, reflecting the increase in exports and progress in inventory adjustments," the BOJ said in its report for May. \par \par \uc2  It is the first time since May-July 2000 that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its view of the economy for three consecutive months. \par \par \uc2  The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (METI) said its all-industries index, a supply-side proxy for gross domestic product, also climbed 1.2 percent on a month-on-month basis after rising 0.4 percent in February. \par \par \uc2  Quarter-on-quarter, the all-industries index was up 0.6 percent, the first increase in four quarters. \par \par \uc2  The data reflects a rebound in the world's second-largest economy that has been driven almost entirely by shipments of automobiles, electronics goods and other key exports to rebounding U.S. and Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  After a three-quarter-long recession from April to December last year - the deepest since World War Two - initial projections are for GDP growth that could be as high as eight or nine percent in January-March on an annualised real-term basis. \par \par \uc2  The GDP figures will be released on June 7, but already economists are talking about Japan outstripping the U.S. growth rate of 5.8 percent for the first quarter of the year. \par \par \uc2  The sustainability of the rebound, however, is being called into question. \par \par \uc2  "Consumption was not bad in January-March but it does not seem to be increasing rapidly," said Kazuhiko Ogata, an economist at HSBC Securities. "The economic recovery lacks strength." \par \par \uc2  CLOUDY OUTLOOK \par \par \uc2  Kazuaki Hasegawa, director of METI's current statistics division, remained cautious despite upbeat data on Wednesday from his own department. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot freely feel joy yet," he told reporters, adding that he has not heard any encouraging news from companies since the start of the new financial year on April 1. \par \par \uc2  About one-third of the tertiary index reflects retailing, which took off in March partly in response to unseasonally warm weather that fuelled a burst of sales in spring clothing. \par \par \uc2  "We saw very good department store sales in March," said UBS Warburg economist Ayako Mitsui. "But we are seeing very bad numbers for April retail sales already. So the tertiary index should dip significantly in the coming month." \par \par \uc2  Adding some risk to the outlook is the yen, which reversed course after falling through the latter half of last year and is now 9.3 percent above a three-year low struck against the dollar on January 31. \par \par \uc2  The yen hit a five-and-a-half month high of 123.50 per dollar earlier in the day, and was standing at 124.84 in afternoon trading after being repelled by what \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said was yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen remains about seven percent below where it stood against the dollar last September. \par \par \uc2  But if recent gains are sustained, economist say the rise could pose a problem for exporters which relied on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's drop last year to gain a competitive edge over offshore rivals and bolster profit margins. \par \par \uc2  Large manufacturers set a dollar/yen rate of an average 124.21 yen when they made their business plans for the year to next March, according to the Bank of Japan's "tankan" corporate survey released in April. \par \par \uc2  In another sign that the outlook is fragile, Japan's 10 leading power utilities generated 2.8 percent less in sales for the year to April than the previous year, an eighth month of year-on-year declines, an industry association said. \par \par \uc2  The April figure was below March, when power sales totalled 65,399 gWh, the Federation of Electric Power Companies said. \par \par \uc2  Power sales to large-lot industrial companies fared better but still remained weak, falling 1.8 percent in April, the 15th straight month of year-on-year declines. \par \par \uc2  J.P. Morgan economist Ryo Hino said the industrial electricity usage implied a gain of 1.4 percent month-on-month, seasonally adjusted, in line with the government's forecast for a 1.3 percent rise in industrial output, month-on-month, in April. \par \par 