\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-SNB condemned to sit back and watch Sfr rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marcel Michelson \par \uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 14:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ZURICH, May 22 (Reuters) - The Swiss National Bank is condemned to sit back and watch the franc rise against the dollar and the euro because any action to halt the franc's advances would be ineffective and may be counterproductive, economists said. \par \par \uc2  As the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive down the strong yen on Wednesday, the three members of the SNB directorate may be gritting their teeth as they see their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move up on dollar weakness and its safe-haven status, while they are hearing loud appeals from exporters for remedial action. \par \par \uc2  Economists said it was highly unlikely that the SNB would resort to open market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The SNB has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1995 when it spent some $200 million to support the dollar in a round of co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The SNB can't do much right now. They just have to wait for this period of uncertainty to pass. They could actually cut official interest rates again, or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally," said Jan Amrit Poser, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Sarasin, adding that he did not expect physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the SNB. "They have been fine tuning on the repo side but the problem is they don't really have any power (to stop the franc rise). The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market may have actually helped more than the repo cut," Poser said. \par \par \uc2  Janwillem Acket, chief economist at Julius Baer, said he expected the SNB to keep its rate cut ammunition dry until either the euro dropped below 1.40 to the franc or the benchmark three-month Swiss franc LIBOR rate was fixed below 1.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The SNB, proud of its independence from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which gives the Alpine republic lower interest rates than those in Euroland, lacks the size to move against the markets in the big pools of dollar and euro liquidity. \par \par \uc2  "They are trying to scoop up the sea with a tin can," said Acket. "They tried dramatic action with the unexpected (half-point) rate cut of May 2 but it proved to be unsuccessful," he added. \par \par \uc2  ANOTHER TWO PIPS \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the SNB sliced two pips from its repo rate to 1.03 percent and is expected to reduce this rate further step by step to alleviate tension on the franc. \par \par \uc2  Later this week or early next, the rate set on the SNB's near-daily short-term money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  could fall below the pyschologically important level of 1.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It clearly shows our desire to keep the money market liquid," an SNB spokesman said when asked about the latest dip in repo rates, which have been falling since late March. \par \par \uc2  Three-month LIBOR , the SNB's main policy focus, eased to 1.24333 percent on Wednesday from 1.28167 percent on Tuesday, slipping below the targeted midpoint of the 0.75-1.75 percent rate band used since the May 2 cut . \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a 28-month low of 1.5665 francs by midday and the euro was trading at 1.4524/29 francs - still above the 1.4489 francs it fell to on May 1. \par \par \uc2  The dollar regained a bit of ground in early trading as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar against the yen. But the impact did not last long. \par \par \uc2  "The main reason is that the confidence is lost, and so there is less money flowing into the U.S. equity market," said Hanspeter Hausheer, senior economist for Switzerland at UBS Warburg in Zurich. \par \par \uc2  "I expect the SNB will continue bringing down its repurchase rates... they will try to keep the euro from going below 1.45 francs," Hausheer said. \par \par \uc2  He was speaking before a bomb scare closed New York's Brooklyn Bridge, heightening fears of another extremist attack on a high-profile U.S. target. The threat of war between India and Pakistan and continued strife in the Middle East also highlighted the safety of tiny, neutral Switzerland until the euro wins more investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  SNB officials have also given clear signals that they would not go against the market and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ineffective. \par \par \uc2  In January, SNB Vice-Chairman Bruno Gehrig told reporters \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "only a measure one would use when nothing else works. Experiences with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are discouraging." \par \par \uc2  Chairman Jean-Pierre Roth said last week the SNB would use the leeway to cut rates which a rising franc affords but stressed rates must come up when the economic recovery was under way. \par \par \uc2  The SNB still has 75 basis points of room for rate cuts, with a range of zero to one percent as the lowest "zero-rate" target possible. But it would be unwise to use that soon. \par \par \uc2  "They cut in May and you see what happened. We are still in May and they better wait," said Acket. \par \par \uc2  - with additional reporting by Alice Ratcliffe and Jon Cox. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020522dy5m00k2m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
