\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-As Japan economy rebounds, yen rise triggers alarm. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jason Szep \par \uc2 854 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 01:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 22 (Reuters) - Japan likely rebounded from recession in the first quarter of 2002, a key government index suggested on Wednesday, propelling Tokyo stock prices to nine-month highs and triggering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the yen surged. \par \par \uc2  The government's all-industries index climbed 0.6 percent in the quarter - its first increase in a year. It rose 1.2 percent in March from the previous month, stronger than most economists had expected. \par \par \uc2  The index, a proxy for gross domestic product, was seen as evidence Japan's long-suffering economy expanded in the first three months of 2002 for the first time in four quarters, putting an end to Japan's worst recession since World War Two. \par \par \uc2  After a three-quarter-long recession from April to December last year - the deepest in 50 years - initial projections are for robust GDP growth of eight or nine percent in January-March on an annualised basis, eclipsing even the United States. \par \par \uc2  "The pace of deterioration in Japan's economy has moderated," the Bank of Japan said in its May report, in which it upgraded its official view of the economy for a third straight month. \par \par \uc2  "Production is starting to pick up, reflecting the increase in exports and progress in inventory adjustments," it said. \par \par \uc2  It is the first time since May-July 2000 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its view of the economy for three consecutive months. \par \par \uc2  The brighter prospects are luring foreign investors back to Tokyo's stock market. \par \par \uc2  Its main Nikkei average climbed 1.36 percent on Wednesday, helping sweep the yen to a five-and-a-half month high against the dollar and triggering yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Recent movements of the exchange rate have been too rapid," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said. "We have taken appropriate action today in the exchange market." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa later sent mixed signals on future action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, first hinting of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise but later saying he hadn't given such instructions. \par \par \uc2  EXPORT-CRIMPING \par \par \uc2  The official unease reflects fears that the yen's rise could cripple a nascent export-led recovery by making Japanese goods less competitive offshore and squeezing earnings at the big exporters credited with turning the economy around. \par \par \uc2  "They are definitely worried about it," said Ron Bevacqua, chief economist at Commerz Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, acting on behalf of the Finance Ministry, sold yen for dollars after the yen traded at a high of 123.5 per dollar - a 9.5 percent increase from three-year lows in late January. \par \par \uc2  But despite official concern over the yen, the president of Japan's biggest automaker, Toyota Motor Corp, said he saw no serious impact if the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades at current levels. \par \par \uc2  "There were times when the yen was much stronger. We don't see a serious impact on our business from the current exchange rate levels," Fujio Cho told reporters. \par \par \uc2  He repeated that a rate of between 110 and 120 yen per dollar would be suitable for his business, which enjoyed record earnings in the last business year to March. \par \par \uc2  Large Japanese manufacturers have set a dollar/yen rate on average at 124.21 yen when making business plans for the year to next March, the Bank of Japan's April "tankan" survey showed. \par \par \uc2  SERVICE INDUSTRY REBOUND \par \par \uc2  In one sign that a recovery in big manufacturers is spilling into the broader economy, a key barometer of service industries rose for the first time in four months in March. \par \par \uc2  The surprisingly strong 1.2 percent gain in the government's tertiary industries index in March - the biggest month-on-month rise in a year - showed service companies in the transport, telecoms and retail sectors starting to recover. \par \par \uc2  But the sustainability of the rebound remains in doubt. \par \par \uc2  "Consumption was not bad in January-March but it does not seem to be increasing rapidly," said Kazuhiko Ogata, an economist at HSBC Securities. "The economic recovery lacks strength." \par \par \uc2  Kazuaki Hasegawa, director of the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry's statistics division, remained cautious despite the upbeat data on service industries compiled by his own department. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot freely feel joy yet," he told reporters, adding that he has not heard any encouraging news from companies since the start of the new financial year on April 1. \par \par \uc2  About one-third of the tertiary index reflects retailing, which took off in March partly in response to unseasonally warm weather that fuelled a burst of sales in spring clothing. \par \par \uc2  "We saw very good department store sales in March," said UBS Warburg economist Ayako Mitsui. "But we are seeing very bad numbers for April retail sales already. So the tertiary index should dip significantly in the coming month." \par \par \uc2  But underscoring the fragility of the recovery, Japan's 10 leading power utilities generated 2.8 percent less in sales for the year to April than the previous year, an eighth month of year-on-year declines, an industry association said. \par \par \uc2  ($1=124.26 Yen) \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Yonggi Kang).