\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-Japan markets rise on recovery hopes, BOJ steps in. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jason Szep \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 12:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 22 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan stepped in to rebuff a rise by the yen to five-month highs against the dollar on Wednesday as Tokyo stocks leapt to their best levels in nine months, swept up by hopes Japan was crawling out of recession. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen for dollars from 123.70/80 and above after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached a five-and-a-half-month high of 123.50 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed days of verbal warnings by Japanese officials who had complained loudly that the yen's rise was happening too quickly. A strong Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the potential to derail \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nascent export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  After the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drove the yen back down, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa confirmed his ministry had taken action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since September. \par \par \uc2  "Recent movements of the exchange rate have been too rapid. We have taken appropriate action today in the exchange market," Shiokawa said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to closely monitor the market and take appropriate action as necessary." \par \par \uc2  The yen-selling steps by the BOJ reflects official unease at recent strength in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has reversed course after falling in the latter half of 2001. It is now 9.3 percent above a three-year low struck against the dollar in January. \par \par \uc2  "They are definitely worried about it," said Ron Bevacqua, chief economist at Commerz Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  If sustained, the rise threatens to unwind the benefits of three straight months climbing exports that are helping to pull Japan out of a three-quarter long recession from April to December, its deepest in 50 years, economists say. \par \par \uc2  "The export cycle has begun to stimulate domestic economic activity. Employment is improving. Overtime hours are up. Profits are improving, setting the stage for a recovery in capital expenditure as well," Bevacqua said. \par \par \uc2  "So the real worry is not that the rising yen is going to take away growth now," he said. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS JUMP \par \par \uc2  Recent signs that Japan's economy has turned the corner during the first three months of 2002 have been underpinning gains on the Tokyo stock market, and the Nikkei 225 share average closed at a nine-month high on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Investors snapped up shares in Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp and other domestic-related issues. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan upgraded its view of the economy on Wednesday for a third straight month as a key barometer of service industries - from sushi restaurants to auto repair shops - rose for the first time in four months. \par \par \uc2  A surprisingly strong 1.2 percent gain in the government's tertiary industries index in March - the biggest month-on-month rise in a year - showed service companies in the transport, telecoms and retail sectors starting to recover. \par \par \uc2  Also, the government's all-industries index climbed 0.6 percent in the three months to March from the previous quarter, its first rise in four quarters. \par \par \uc2  Volume on the Tokyo Stock Exchange shot up on Wednesday, with more than one billion shares changing hands on the first section for only the 11th time in 2002. \par \par \uc2  "There's definitely foreign buying going on," said Hamish Ross, head of trading at Instinet Japan. "Everyone's realising it's time to move money into banks and construction and retail...everything that says domestic demand." \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average closed up 1.36 percent or 160.82 points at 11,961.98 while the capital-weighted TOPIX index climbed 1.19 percent to 1,129.67, also a nine-month high. \par \par \uc2  Sumitomo Mitsui, Japan's second-largest banking group, shot up 5.9 percent to 681 yen as the most active issue in value terms. Banks will unveil massive losses for 2001/02 on Friday, but hopes are an economic recovery will ease their bad-loan woes. \par \par \uc2  Strength in the stock market drew investors away from the debt market, which ended mixed, but an auction of 10-year bonds drew strong support, reflecting a belief interest that rates will stay low this year. Short term rates are already virtually zero. \par \par \uc2  Bids on the bonds totalled a record 13.4603 trillion yen ($108 billion). \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Nathan Layne) ($1=124.26 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020522dy5m00b52\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
