\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Asian stocks sturdy, dollar dips, gold glistens. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Bill Tarrant \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 09:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 22 (Reuters) - Fresh signs of regional recovery drove Asia's key stock markets higher on Wednesday, while the dollar swooned and gold glittered after the FBI warned of possible terror attacks against New York landmarks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a nearly six-month low against the yen, before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  reined in the galloping yen with an afternoon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold spurted to a 27-month high of $317.50 an ounce in Asia, as investors scrambled for a safe-haven asset with war fears mounting on the India-Pakistan border, tensions deepening in the Middle East and terror warnings in the United States. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares closed at a nine-month high with banks, builders and consumer issues in the money after a string of data, including March industrial output on Wednesday, offered more evidence the worst is behind the world's second-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei average closed up 1.36 percent at 11,961.98 after the all-industries index posted a 0.6 percent rise in the first quarter, its first increase in four quarters. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone's realising it's time to move money into banks, construction and retail...everything that says domestic demand," said Hamish Ross, head of trading at Instinet Japan. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN MOVES ON YEN \par \par \uc2  But the yen's strength sapped exporters, such as makers of cars and electronics, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Japan jumped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to put the brakes on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Recent movements of the exchange rate have been too rapid," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said in a statement. "We have taken appropriate action today in the exchange market." \par \par \uc2  At 0630 GMT, the dollar was at 124.70/yen, after hitting the 123-level earlier in the day, and was at $0.9212/euro from $0.9200 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainties about U.S. markets and America's mounting current account deficit have kept pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Asia-Pacific stock markets traded against the U.S. trend for a change. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks took a dive after a law enforcement official said New York City may issue alerts about possible terror threats to the Brooklyn Bridge and the Statue of Liberty over the long Memorial Day weekend at the end of May. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average fell 1.21 percent to 10,105.71 while the technology-laced Nasdaq Composite Index slipped 2.2 percent to 1,664.18. \par \par \uc2  AUSTRALIA, KOREA, TAIWAN UP Australian stocks gained as gold and financials emerged as defensive plays, taking heed of the U.S. jitters without being handcuffed to Wall Street's weakness. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index closed up 0.38 percent to 3389.8 with mining stocks again favoured as gold prices spike. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index closed up 3.04 percent at 863.06, after official figures showed South Korea's economy grew 5.7 percent year on year in the first quarter. The index has gained some 23 percent so far this year. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan stocks turned around following an eight percent drop the previous four sessions, as computer-related issues clawed back some of their losses. The benchmark TAIEX closed up 1.81 percent at 5541.64. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks were up a tad by mid-afternoon as hopes for an economic recovery and strong fund inflows made for a cheery outlook, analysts said. By 0630 GMT the Hang Seng Index was up 0.15 percent at 11,770.39. \par \par \uc2  Singapore stocks were also generally flat, amid concerns about the U.S. economy, the island's biggest market. But optimism about the local economy helped push the benchmark Straits Times Index up 0.13 percent to 1732.79 by 0630 GMT. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020522dy5m00asy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
