\par\par \b \uc2  As Yen Surges, BOJ Increases Its Calls To  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Banks \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2002\par 16:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The yen's rapid strengthening against both the euro and the dollar Tuesday has prompted an increased number of calls to New York and London-based banks by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but this doesn't necessarily signal that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is imminent, according to several market sources. \par \par \uc2  "I can confirm that the BOJ directly has been calling," said an analyst at one major bank, adding that the call was "to get a feeling for what the reasons are for the move." \par \par \uc2  "They may be trying to find out if the strength of yen represents genuine interest in Japanese assets or purely a speculative attempt to drive the yen stronger," he said. \par \par \uc2  Other market participants, who asked not to be named, also confirmed that the Bank of Japan has been making more frequent and lengthy calls in a bid to clarify whether the yen's rapid movement represents a continuation of the continued downward trend in the dollar, or other independent factors. \par \par \uc2  Another person familiar with the BOJ calls said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to find out "what market participants feel about the movement," although he added that his sense was that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains unlikely. Indeed, the yen continued to rally even as these calls were being made, suggesting that the inquiries didn't raise fears that the BOJ would try to stop the gains. \par \par \uc2  The calls were made from both the New York and London representative offices of the BOJ, sources said. \par \par \uc2  The yen jumped to almost six-month highs against the dollar earlier Tuesday in New York, and also surged against the euro, in a move that some analysts say came on the back of fund managers snapping up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a growing aversion to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rapid fall against the yen over recent weeks - and in particular over the past few days - has put market participants on heightened alert for BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with some justification. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the guardian of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, has made it quite clear that it doesn't favor a strong yen, largely because of the strains it would place on Japanese exporters and the risk that it could undermine a cyclical recovery in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who rarely comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, even weighed in on the debate overnight in Tokyo, noting that while the yen's sharp rise shows Japan's economy is doing better than expected, rapid movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  aren't good. Koizumi's remarks were taken as a sign that Tokyo is ready to fight any speculative rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen by over two yen Tuesday, a move that comes on the heels of a similarly large loss on Friday. The dollar is now down 3.4% against its Japanese counterpart since its high on that day. \par \par \uc2  Late morning in New York, the dollar was changing hands at Y123.79, its weakest level since Dec. 6, 2001 and sharply down from its high for the day of Y126.07. The euro was trading at Y113.67, its lowest point since March, 13. \par \par \uc2  Just how much effect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have now isn't clear, however, particularly as the yen's surge is coming amid generalized dollar weakness and apparently driven more by real investment flows to Japan than speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases. "With real money flows reinforcing this move, the BOJ and MOF are quite reluctant to actually participate in direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, while the BOJ has had some measure of success in the past with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - most notably in the aftermath of the Sept. 11 attacks which prompted a wave of yen buying - analysts continue to believe that stepping into the market now to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might only have a limited and short-term effect because it would go against the tide of market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  As well as the general move to sell the dollar, the yen is also getting a lift from some glimmers of optimism in Japan itself, despite the widespread view that the world's second largest economy remains beset by a slew of structural problems, particularly the weight of non-performing loans in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Japan's recent industrial production data showed "some improvement...coming out of deep pessimism," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Putnam Investments in Boston. The economy "looks like it is doing a little better," allowing "some tolerance for the yen to strengthen," \par \par \uc2  The yen has also been boosted by fund managers increasing the weighting of Japanese stocks in their portfolios. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is set to remain extremely focussed on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes Tuesday's phone calls all the more significant. Moreover, the fact that the yen buying has now shifted to include purchases made against the euro has led some analysts to suggest that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be more amenable to cooperating in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (John Parry contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020521dy5l005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
