\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar stays under pressure, near five-month lows. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2002\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 20 (Reuters) - The dollar stayed under pressure in Tokyo on Monday, near five-month lows against the yen, on doubts over the sustainability of the U.S. economic rebound, while the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefited from Tokyo's resurgent stock market. The dollar also threatened fresh five-month lows on the euro after stronger-than-expected U.S. economic indicators failed to boost the greenback last week. \par \par \uc2  The yen clung to hefty gains struck Friday after Deputy Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Masahiro Kawai said Japan had no intention of driving the yen artificially lower. \par \par \uc2  His comments, in contrast with an official line that the current economic fundamentals do not warrant a stronger yen, nudged the dollar to 125.52 yen offshore on Friday, a 2002 low. \par \par \uc2  Dealers increasingly doubt that Japanese authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen makes steady inroads against the dollar as long as the gains are at a moderate pace. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, for example, the ministry's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said Japan is ready to take appropriate action as needed, but the market largely ignored his remarks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers reasoned it would be difficult for Japanese authorities to carry out dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  while blaming \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weak fundamentals, when the government itself admitted the economy had hit a bottom. \par \par \uc2  Late on Friday Japan's Cabinet Office released its monthly report, in which it upgraded its economic view, saying the economy had levelled out. \par \par \uc2  "Unless the dollar falls close to the 120 yen level, I don't think there will be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yoshiyasu Naruse, senior manager for treasury sales at HSBC in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The dollar eased to around 125.70 yen, down from 125.94 yen late in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN RECOVERY? \par \par \uc2  The yen was also helped by a firm tone in Japanese stock market, which ended higher for a fifth straight session. The Nikkei average rose 0.8 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  "Many foreign investors who were underweight Japanese stocks are now returning their portfolios to neutral," said Yasuharu Tsuru, manager at Mitsubishi Trust and banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  "There's also speculation that Japan's first quarter gross domestic products (GDP) could be quite strong, adding support for the yen," Tsuru also said. \par \par \uc2  January-March quarter GDP, due on June 7, is widely expected to show annualised growth of around 8.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  Speculators, both Japanese and foreign, are trying to test the downside, especially key support at 125.50 - half a point between the post-September 11 low and high. \par \par \uc2  That level was also considered a crucial trigger for option-related stop-loss selling, a break of which could send the greenback below 124 yen, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  But some said the dollar's fall would be limited as Japanese investors are eager to snap up dollars on dips. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese institutional investors are intent on increasing foreign investments. Individual investors are also likely to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on dips," said Kazunari Kobayashi, treasury manager at State Street Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was mostly quiet in Asia but was holding at 92.04 cents, near its five-month peak around 92.19 hit in offshore trade. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood at 115.80 yen, against 115.90 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed to capitalise on a stronger-than-expected reading in University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey and current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has been sold as investors diversify their funds on hopes for a global recovery," said Kobayashi of State Street. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling eked out gains to $1.4619 from $1.4573. Dealers see its gains as an adjustment to its recent fall due to speculation that Britain was moving a step closer to EMU entry. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0605 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2  17 May. \par \par \uc2  ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr       0.9206   0.9203   +0.03    +3.39   0.8904\par Dlr/yen        125.77   125.94   -0.13    -4.45   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/yen    115.79   115.90   -0.09    -1.24   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dlr/swiss      1.5790   1.5800   -0.06    -4.85   1.6594\par Stg/dlr        1.4621   1.4573   +0.33    +0.55   1.4541\par Can/dlr        1.5458   1.5434   +0.16    -2.88   1.5917\par Aus/dlr        0.5515   0.5525   -0.18    +8.01   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/swiss  1.4538   1.4545   -0.05    -1.64   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/stg    0.6293   0.6318   -0.40    +2.78   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ..  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\u