r \uc2  A falling dollar runs the risk of scaring foreign investors from dollar-denominated asset markets, driving them lower, which in turn would put fresh pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A weaker dollar could also lead to inflation, because it would make imported goods more expensive, adding another concern for investors. \par \par \uc2  The extent of the dollar's broad vulnerability was particularly apparent Friday, when it plummeted against the yen and the euro and slid against a clutch of widely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  clearly worsening, the dollar dropped to five-month lows against the yen, while the euro traded above 92 cents, seeing its best levels since October 2001. \par \par \uc2  The dollar may hold around these levels or even weaken further against both its two major rivals this week, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists suggested. \par \par \uc2  For the euro to maintain its gains around the 92-cent mark wouldn't be surprising, said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist and head of North American research at the Bank of New York. \par \par \uc2  "There is a longer-term trend in place that the euro and all other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be slowly appreciating against the U.S. dollar," as negative sentiment on the greenback intensifies, Woolfolk added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts are now becoming more insistent about a long-standing argument, that the current account deficit - around 4% of gross domestic product now but widening - poses a grave risk to dollar-denominated assets. \par \par \uc2  And unless there is unexpected strength in the week's U.S. economic data, which include a second reading of first quarter GDP and last month's durable goods orders report, expectations that the pace of the U.S. economic rebound is likely to lessen as the year unfolds will continue to weigh on the greenback. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Yen Nears Japan's Pain Threshold\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After the dollar crashed below the Y126 mark in its plunge Friday, the\par possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in an effort to weaken the yen is now much more likely, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par During recent weeks, most strategists have cited an area between\par around Y125 and Y120 as the "discomfort zone," which would make the BOJ,\par acting on behalf of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , very likely to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It seems like the Y125 handle is at the beginning of their pain\par threshold," and this next week \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders are likely to\par "to push buttons and see where the Bank of Japan would get a little\par worried," said Ed Stapleton, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Fortis USA\par Financial Markets in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese government wants to see the yen weaken against other\par major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to make the country's goods cheaper to sell\par abroad. By so doing it would aim to nurture some incipient economic\par growth in Japan's otherwise stagnant economy that has manifested\par themselves recently in the country's export sector.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among the widely traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the non-U.S. dollars are expected\par to remain robust.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Australian dollar hit fresh 15-month highs Friday, while the Swiss\par franc - a traditional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of refuge when the greenback shows\par fragility - reached its highest levels against the dollar since late\par September 2001.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ascent of the so-called commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , so named because\par market participants view the economic fortunes of Canada, Australia and\par New Zealand as quite closely correlated to the direction of commodities\par prices, is much more than a short-term bounce, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders\par added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Effectively, these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are getting their second wind. All of\par them "have been strengthening against the dollar for a longer time than\par the euro and the yen have," Mazanec noted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In late trading in New York Friday, the euro was at $0.9208, up from\par $0.9131 in London and above $0.9119 late Thursday in New York. Against\par the yen, the dollar was at Y125.87, down from Y126.88 in London and\par below Y128.09 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at CHF1.5785\par down from CHF1.5971 Thursday and the pound was at $1.4594 up from\par $1.4570.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com;\par 201-938-2096\par    (Michael Casey and John Hardy contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djan000020020519dy5j0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
