\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-No plan to weaken yen artificially-MOF official. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2002\par 15:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 17 (Reuters) - Japan has no intention of driving the yen lower artificially, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Friday, giving traders the green light to drive the yen to the year's highs against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities are thought to be uncomfortable with the recently firming yen, preferring relative weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to stimulate exports and domestic growth. \par \par \uc2  But Masahiro Kawai, deputy Vice Minister for International Finance at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cast doubt on this view, saying in the text of a speech at a conference in London: \par \par \uc2  "They (the government and the Bank of Japan) clearly reject the idea of resorting to a beggar-thy-neighbour \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of driving the yen artificially lower." \par \par \uc2  The yen soared to five-month highs against the dollar around 126 on the comments, shrugging off later remarks from top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy had not changed, and that the country was watching to see if exchange rates reflect econonmic fundamentals and would take appropriate action on the yen if needed. \par \par \uc2  Kawai said in the speech that the yen's slide from October to January had been a market-driven phenomenon reflecting Japan's poor fundamentals and lax monetary policy. "It is not a result of the authorities' deliberate manipulation to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  value down. Indeed the government and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have avoided \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  expect for the very brief period immediately after the September 11 event." \par \par \uc2  The yen has recovered in recent months, making traders nervous that the authorities will step in to prevent it damaging export sales. \par \par \uc2  "When the economy begins to recover the yen is expected to appreciate because of its long-standing, strong external position. After all Japan has persistent current account surpluses and is one of the largest net creditor nations in the world. Therefore a weak yen will not continue indefinitely," he said. \par \par \uc2  Kawai told Reuters on the sidelines of the conference, organised by ratings agency Fitch, that the yen's recent strength was not a cause for concern. \par \par \uc2  "We would be concerned if the yen's exchange rate deviated from fundamentals extremely. The recent moves reflect market psychology and the outlook for the U.S. economy," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020517dy5h00psc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
