\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr's Fall Below Y126 Makes BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  More Likely \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2002\par 18:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- After the dollar has crashed through some key technical support levels in its plunge earlier Friday, the possibility of some type of action from the Bank of Japan in an effort to weaken the yen is now much more likely, U.S.-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar dropped below a key level that was just above Y126, there is a noticeably big gap before the next technical level down, at around Y122.65, said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist and head of North American research at the Bank of New York. \par \par \uc2  At midday  in New York, the dollar was at Y125.98. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ is aware of and respects those technical levels," and if the dollar continues to fall against the yen, "I would expect to hear something from the BOJ in the next few trading sessions," Woolfolk added. \par \par \uc2  What remains unclear is at what point an intensification of Japanese officials rhetoric designed to weaken the yen would give way to actual purchases on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  During recent weeks, most strategists have cited an area between around Y125 and Y120 as the "discomfort zone"  which would make the Japanese government very likely to undertake transactions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  In an open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ acts on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through Japanese banks during Asian trading, but has the option of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2   at any time during global trading through its accounts with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Federal Reserve Bank of New York or a national \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Somewhat paradoxically, even the increased possibility of such a step could itself act as a check on the dollar's slippage against the yen, noted Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  The greenback "could stay weaker (against the yen) near term," but at the same time, "the dollar could be underpinned by the view that MOF will act to stem further weakness" as that will affect the positions that market participants take, Lynch said. \par \par \uc2  Central banks have generally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets in attempts to slow a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's movement in a direction the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deems unfavorable, to reinforce movement in the direction it favors or to restore a normal trading environment to a market made disorderly by extremely large and rapid movements. \par \par \uc2  Japan has the world's largest foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped into markets in recent years far more often than the ECB or the Fed. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government doesn't want the yen to appreciate too far against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to keep the country's goods competitive on foreign markets. The government aims to nurture the few incipient stirrings of economic growth in the country's export sector that have manifested themselves recently in Japan's otherwise stagnant economy. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096 (John Hardy contributed to\par this report).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020517dy5h006y3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
