\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar claws back ground ahead of Wall Street. \b0\par\par\uc2 772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2002\par 14:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  By James Thornhill LONDON, May 13 (Reuters) - The dollar clawed back lost ground to make modest gains against the euro and yen on Monday as U.S. stock futures pointed to an improved tone ahead of Wall Street's opening. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on, the dollar had been weak, hovering within spitting distance of recent seven-month lows against the euro and the Swiss franc and within a yen of a two-month trough, hit by sharp falls on Wall Street on Friday. \par \par \uc2  However U.S. stock futures showed modest gains, helping to turn the greenback's fortunes around. \par \par \uc2  "It's been a quiet morning...The dollar is firm across the board and firmer stock futures are helping it," said Steven Pearson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Halifax group treasury. \par \par \uc2  By 1215 GMT the dollar was slightly above Friday's closing levels at 127.67 yen, and almost a quarter percent higher at $0.9117 per euro and 1.5946 Swiss francs. \par \par \uc2  The yen had led the way higher earlier in Asia with the market shrugging off expectations that rating agency Moody's Investor Service would downgrade Japan when results of a long-awaited review are released this week. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, vice finance minister for international affairs, Haruhiko Kuroda further doused fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell the strength of the yen, saying he would leave the market to decide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. The yen traded a touch firmer against the euro at 116.40. \par \par \uc2  WALL STREET IN THE SPOTLIGHT \par \par \uc2  Monday's trade in Europe was subdued with many in the market waiting to see how U.S. stocks perform after the rollercoaster ride they went on previous week. \par \par \uc2  A 3.0 percent fall in the high-tech Nasdaq index and a 0.97 percent decline in the blue-chip Dow Jones on Friday erased huge stock market gains made mid-week on the back of an upbeat quarterly report from U.S. giant Cisco Systems. \par \par \uc2  A raft of U.S. data this week suggests that Wall Street could be in for more volatility this week. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of data this week and I don't expect it to be particularly supportive, giving further reason to believe that U.S. growth momentum is slowing in the second quarter," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  U.S. April retail sales data, due out on Tuesday, are forecast to post a healthy 0.6 percent rise, but that could be offset by weak production numbers on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  April consumer price data is also due on Wednesday, while trade data on Friday will remind the market about the size of the country's deficit. \par \par \uc2  EURO RISKS \par \par \uc2  Wall Street is sure to be a key focus this week, but some analysts also point to growing risks for the near-term euro outlook. \par \par \uc2  "There has been a large move in euro/dollar in the last few weeks and we could now be looking at a period of consolidation," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  Speculators on Chicago's IMM \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures set a record long euro position for the third week running last week, adding to fears that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be setting itself up for a sharp reversal. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of the move in euro/dollar has been driven by disappointing U.S. economic indicators, but we've also seen weakness in euro zone indicators," Shea said. \par \par \uc2  However, the market continued to pay scant attention to uninspiring economic data from the region. \par \par \uc2  In the euro zone's biggest economy, Germany, March retail sales rose in line with expectations at 1.2 percent on the month, but economists said the data was not yet a sign of a turnaround in consumer spending. \par \par \uc2  German April inflation slowed to a 1.6 percent annual rate, from 1.8 percent, but still showed signs of a build-up in price pressures in heating oil and fuel, and prices in the services sector. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week euro zone inflation would ease in months ahead but added that oil prices, wage negotiations and the introduction of euro notes and coins in January had clouded price prospects. \par \par \uc2  The comments firmed market expectations that the ECB may raise interest rates sooner rather than later in the year despite signs of only sluggish recovery in the region. \par \par \uc2  It was a good day for high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Australian dollar hitting 15-month highs against its U.S. counterpart, the New Zealand $21-month highs and the Norwegian crown two-year highs. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020513dy5d00p01\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
