\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar trims gains on yen, reprieve short-lived. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2002\par 08:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 8 (Reuters) - The dollar gave up earlier gains against the yen on Wednesday, having trouble extending its rally from two-month lows on modest selling from Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  While helped by a steadier Wall Street and non-stop jawboning from Japanese authorities, the dollar succumbed to exporter selling at 127.91 yen in late Asia after an earlier climb near offshore peaks of 128.39. \par \par \uc2  That had compared with a two-month low of 126.76 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very short of dollar, and we're seeing time ripe for some adjustment," said a trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "But whether that'll see the dollar gain more than two or three yen from here is questionable," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to leave rates and its bias unchanged was exactly as expected and had little impact on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the dollar's latest bounce came from active buying from U.S. funds caught short of dollars, as well as talk Japanese pension funds were buying into foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  Japan's finance ministry also kept up a verbal campaign to stem a rise in the yen to help keep export competitiveness intact. \par \par \uc2  Its financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, repeated he was watching the market closely and that the Finance Ministry stood ready to act if needed. \par \par \uc2  Though his predecessor, Eisuke Sakakibara, later said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not very likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 125 yen, his comments went little noticed given his waning influence on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. \par \par \uc2  The euro was a touch softer on the dollar, trading at $0.9135 from $0.9149 in late New York, while on the yen it was pressed by exporters at 116.84 yen after hitting one-month highs around 117.23 offshore. \par \par \uc2  SHORT-TERM RELIEF \par \par \uc2  The dollar was helped by surprisingly good U.S. productivity data and an upbeat after-hours earnings report from Wall Street bellwether Cisco Systems which saw Nasdaq futures jump a hefty 25 points. \par \par \uc2  U.S. non-farm productivity in the first quarter advanced at a blistering rate of 8.6 percent - its largest gain in nearly 19 years and far faster than the 6.9 percent rise expected - implying few inflationary pressures and improving corporate profit margins. \par \par \uc2  Support for the dollar also came from talk Japanese pension funds were wading into the market to buy foreign bonds, with talk rife a few Japanese funds had shown strong interest in the Treasury's $33 billion quarterly refunding this week. \par \par \uc2  Some of the buying was suspected to be unhedged and thus dollar-positive. \par \par \uc2  "We'll probably see a steady stream of non-hedged foreign bond bond buying from Japanese individual investors and pension funds going forward," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  "That, combined with the possibility for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will keep the dollar well-supported," he said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasuries have performed strongly of late on the Fed's uncertain view on the economy, helping restore some confidence in U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Still, many saw the dollar's gains limited, given doubts over Wall Street's direction and the growing allure of higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Canadian and Australian dollars. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie dollar proved the best performer for the day, helped by Japanese demand for Aussie/yen after the Reserve Bank of Australia raised rates as expected but sounded more bullish on the economy than many anticipated. \par \par \uc2  In reaction, the market has moved swiftly to price in a more aggressive tightening cycle which promises to give the Aussie an increasing yield advantage. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0620 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 07 May.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9131   0.9149   -0.20    +2.55   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen               128.00   127.91   +0.07    -2.76   131.63\par  Euro/yen              116.93   117.07   -0.12    -0.26   117.24\par  Dlr/swiss             1.5936   1.5881   +0.35    -3.97   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr               1.4652   1.4673   -0.14    +0.76   1.4541\par  Can/dlr               1.5671   1.5666   +0.03    -1.55   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr               0.5428   0.5390   +0.71    +6.31   0.5106\par  Euro/swiss            1.4564   1.4531   +0.23    -1.46   1.4780\par  Euro/stg              0.6233   0.6232   +0.02    +1.80   0.6123.\par \par \par \par     For full report on major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ..        Debt...      Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight