\par\par \b \uc2  JGBs edge down on Nikkei, focus on auction results. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2002\par 05:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 8 (Reuters) - Japanese government bond prices edged down on Wednesday morning, reflecting sharp gains in Tokyo shares, but the market was reluctant to take new positions ahead of confirming the results of a five-year JGB auction. \par \par \uc2  The JGB market retained a bullish trend but operators were nervous about chasing prices up further as long-dated JGBs were already at high levels, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There are many investors wanting to buy JGBs on price dips, but they are not so interested in buying them strongly now when prices are already high," said Kunihiro Ishibashi, a director at Mizuho Investors Securities' bond section. \par \par \uc2  Ishibashi said investors are hoping that they will get chances to buy JGBs at lower prices, particularly after a possible downgrading of Japan by Moody's Investors Service, which the market is heavily speculating will happen soon. \par \par \uc2  Speculation over possible downgrading of Japan's domestic ratings has surfaced in the market repeatedly since another major ratings firm, Standard & Poor's, downgraded JGBs by a notch to AA-minus (AA-) last month, traders said. \par \par \uc2  YEN AND STOCKS \par \par \uc2  Besides possible downgrading, the market saw gains in domestic share prices and the yen's fall as reasons to reap profits in JGBs on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei average rose 2.06 percent or 233.07 points to 11,549.11 on Wednesday morning after diving 2.03 percent a day earlier, its biggest percentage drop in a month. \par \par \uc2  The yen backed off to 128.05 per dollar on Wednesday morning following verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda. He repeated his warning that the government was ready to take appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as needed. \par \par \uc2  Only about 24 hours ago, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit to a fresh two-month high of 126.76. \par \par \uc2  June 10-year JGB futures closed the morning session down 0.12 point at 138.78 after rising to their highest close since late December of 138.90 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the 238th 10-year JGB rose to 1.365 percent, up one basis point from Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  FIVE-YEAR JGB AUCTION \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry offered 1.9 trillion yen ($14.83 billion) in five-year JGBs with a coupon rate of 0.5 percent. The offer was reopened and merged with the 19th five-year JGBs. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect the auction to draw healthy demand with regional investors and city banks showing interest. \par \par \uc2  "The auction is expected to draw strong demand. I don't think the auction will turn out to ruin the present bullish mood in the market," Mizuho Investors' Ishibashi said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the 19th five-year JGBs rose 0.01 percentage point to 0.490 percent from Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the current account balance at the Bank of Japan fell to about 15.6 trillion yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbed about 500 billion yen from the money market at its regular 0020 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ($1=128.15 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020508dy58005em\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
