\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Kuroda keeps up verbal  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  on  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2002\par 03:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, kept up his verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday, saying his ministry was ready to take appropriate action as needed. \par \par \uc2  Asked his view on the dollar's recovery against the yen overnight, Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters: "I will not comment on daily movements. There is no change in our \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy; we are watching markets carefully and are ready to take appropriate action as needed." \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday morning, Kuroda said his ministry was ready to act if necessary - the first time in a while he had used that phrase, a form of official speak that sometimes precedes market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  His repeated warning came even after the dollar had pared some losses overnight after hitting a two-month low of 126.76 in Tokyo on Tuesday. In early Asian trade on Wednesday, the dollar was quoted around 128.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said the most important task for the Japanese economy was to emerge out of deflation. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is bottoming out but prices continue to fall. The Bank of Japan (BOJ) and the government need to work together to push forward anti-deflation steps," he said. \par \par \uc2  "I want the BOJ to continue monetary easing. The government will promote the disposal of banks' bad loans and structural reforms." \par \par \uc2  Asked whether he wanted to see further monetary easing by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kuroda said: "The BOJ decides monetary policy. I hope it will act appropriately as needed." \par \par \uc2  He did not specify what type of action the BOJ should take. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda dismissed the idea of using a weaker yen to help stop deflation, saying monetary policy and the government's anti-deflation steps should do that job. \par \par \uc2  "It is desirable for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move in a stable manner while reflecting economic fundamentals," he said. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen could theoretically ease deflationary pressure by pushing up Japan's import prices. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020508dy58002h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
