\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Kuroda: To Take Proper  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Steps As Needed \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2002\par 02:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to take steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Wednesday, suggesting the ministry may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen rises too far against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is unchanged. We're prepared to take proper \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps as needed," said Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  Reflecting the ministry's wariness over the recent yen surge, Kuroda said the finance ministry is closely watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  He added that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, including the yen, should reflect economic fundamentals and move stably. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has weakened against the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions amid concern over U.S. corporate earnings and the pace of the U.S. economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  In Asian trade Tuesday, the dollar fell to a fresh two-month low of Y126.76 as investors brushed off warnings by Kuroda and other officials that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, however, rallied later in the global day and was trading around Y128.22 at 0103 GMT Wednesday, up from Y127.90 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  While Japan's economy is showing some signs of bottoming out, Kuroda said the nation remains in the grip of deflation. He added that the government and the Bank of Japan will continue to cooperate to fight this pressure weighing on the economy. \par \par \uc2  "It is most important for Japan to overcome deflation," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  To help stem falling domestic prices, Kuroda said he hopes the Bank of Japan will maintain its current super-loose monetary policy and take more easing steps. \par \par \uc2  However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to heed his call as both the BOJ and the government have upgraded their economic assessments for two months in a row amid signs of improvement among manufacturers and a modest easing of deflation. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par ron.harui@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020508dy580000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
