\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar fights back across board ahead of FOMC. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2002\par 14:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 7 (Reuters) - The dollar staged a sharp comeback after hitting two-month lows on the yen and seven-month troughs versus European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, as investors rushed to buy it back on returning from long weekends in London and Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But sentiment for the dollar, which has lost four percent on traded weighted terms in recent weeks, remained fragile as weak U.S. stocks on Monday and recent poor data undermined faith in the prospects for a solid and sustained U.S. recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Given the market's extreme positioning, the dollar cannot continue to fall in the near term," said Steven Saywell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "But the big picture remains unchanged. The dollar is vulnerable as the U.S. will find it increasingly difficult to fund its current account deficit and the market is questioning U.S. asset prices." \par \par \uc2  Worries about the shape of the U.S. recovery have reinforced investor expectations that the Federal Reserve will keep interest rates on hold at its policy-setting committee meeting later on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In midsession European trading the dollar bounced more than one percent against the yen to 128.37 from a low of 126.77. Dealers said concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew given a rising tide of warnings against export-damaging yen strength from Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials. \par \par \uc2  "The market got a little short on dollar positions as well and they're being squeezed out of those positions," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise against the yen helped pull the greenback off its seven-month lows of $0.9189 and 1.5828 Swiss francs to climb more than half a percent up on the day against both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro set a one-month high against the yen of 117.26. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS AND PRODUCTIVITY \par \par \uc2  The Fed is to announce its monetary policy decision at around 1815 GMT on Tuesday and analysts expect rates to be kept at 1.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Before the decision, the market is keeping a wary eye on Wall Street, following Monday's two-percent falls in the Dow Jones and the Nasdaq. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the market could get some heartening news from U.S. productivity figures due later. Average forecasts are for a rise of around 6.9 percent annualised in the first quarter, with unit labour costs expected to fall 3.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  This could bode well for future corporate profits, but analysts say there is no guarantee of a revival on Wall Street, where developments are seen as a key driver for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "High productivity data might help the dollar but the issue here is equity markets," Saywell said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock futures were marginally higher ahead of Wall Street's opening. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the euro showed no reaction to German industrial orders data, which fell 0.1 percent month on month in March against forecasts for a rise of 1.4 percent. Data also showed the number of Germans out of work rose unexpectedly by 6,000 in April to 3.974 million on a seasonally adjusted basis. \par \par \uc2  "Today's data were disappointing and they raised some doubts the eurozone growth rate would accelerate into the second quarter," said Hiroshi Shiraishi, economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  AND \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Halpenny said Japanese investors particularly had been buying dollars against the yen after returning from their Golden Week holiday on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Seeking to sap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda said there was no need for the yen to rise and that the MOF was ready to act if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted this was the first time in a while that Kuroda had used the phrase "ready to act", a form of words that has preceded \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. \par \par \uc2  They added an earlier statement from Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa sounded realistic. He said he had not instructed his staff to act and was waiting on a report from them before deciding. \par \par \uc2  There were signs Japanese institutions have stepped up foreign bond buying with official data showing net purchases worth a surprisingly large 1.17 trillion yen for the week of April 22-26 against just 103 billion the week before. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said this could revive speculation about a flood of funds out of Japan. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020507dy5700ns3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
