\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar wins some reprieve before FOMC. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2002\par 12:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 7 (Reuters) - The dollar regained its balance after hitting two-month lows against the yen and seven-month troughs versus the euro on Tuesday, as investors bought it back on their return from a long weekend in London. \par \par \uc2  But sentiment for the dollar, which has lost four percent on traded weighted terms in recent weeks, remained fragile as weak U.S. stocks on Monday and recent poor data undermined faith in the prospects for a solid and sustained U.S. recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Given the market's extreme positioning, the dollar cannot continue to fall in the near term," said Steven Saywell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "But the big picture remains unchanged. The dollar is vulnerable as the U.S. will it find increasingly difficult to fund its current account deficit and the market is questioning U.S. asset prices." \par \par \uc2  Worries about the shape of the U.S. recovery have reinforced investor expectations that the Federal Reserve will keep interest rates on hold at its policy-setting committee meeting later on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback also drew some support from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warnings against export-damaging yen strength from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials. \par \par \uc2  All of which was enough to pull the greenback off lows against the yen to 127.71 having touched 126.77. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also hit seven month lows against the Swiss franc around 1.5828 before trimming losses to 1.5920. The euro set a one-month high against the yen of 116.83. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, it rose to $0.9143 from a low of $0.9189. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed no reaction to German industrial orders data, which fell 0.1 percent month on month in March. Forecasts had been for a rise of 1.4 percent. Earlier, data showed the number of Germans out of work rose unexpectedly by 6,000 in April to 3.974 million on a seasonally adjusted basis. \par \par \uc2  Leading OECD indicators for the industrial world rose for the fifth consecutive month in March, showing a slower paced rise from February for the United States and a fractionally faster pace in growth for the eurozone. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS AND PRODUCTIVITY \par \par \uc2  U.S. asset markets are becoming increasingly unattractive following Monday's two-percent falls in the Dow Jones and the Nasdaq. \par \par \uc2  "As long as the U.S. stock markets remain weak there's little chance for the dollar to recover. Losses on Wall Street may suggest that U.S. economic prospects are not as bright as we expected a few weeks ago," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, European Economist at CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  The Fed is to announce its rate decision at around 1815 GMT on Tuesday and analysts expect rates to be kept at 1.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  They say the market could get some heartening news from U.S. productivity figures due later. Average forecasts are for a rise of around 6.9 percent annualised in the first quarter, with unit labour costs expected to fall 3.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  This could bode well for future corporate profits, but analysts say there is no guarantee of a revival on Wall Street, where developments are seen as the key driver for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "High productivity data might help the dollar but the issue here is equity markets. So if they react positively it would be the catalyst for the dollar correction," Saywell said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  AND \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par 