\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Is Futile Says O'Neill, Does  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Agree? \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,017 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2002\par 22:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- As the dollar deepens its retreat against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders are again on heightened alert for the possibility the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in an attempt to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  But the multi-billion dollar question \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants are asking - as always when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks may be in the offing - is whether such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases would have their desired effect. \par \par \uc2  Absolutely not, said Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill Wednesday, who articulated the oft-made case in an appearance before the U.S. Senate that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy rests firmly on the inability of governments to influence \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. He even went so far as to argue that grand political pacts to move exchange rates - such as the 1985 Plaza Accord to weaken the dollar - generally don't work. \par \par \uc2  Many U.S.-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists agree with him, largely due to the climate of generalized dollar weakness and a gathering sense that a confluence of negative factors conspiring against the greenback would likely make it difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to influence the market. \par \par \uc2  For the moment, many expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the guardian of yen policy - to step up verbal efforts designed to send the yen lower, but to wait and see whether the dollar's slide gets checked before the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaches unacceptable levels from domestic exporters' point of view. \par \par \uc2  "If the yen is one of many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are moving in the same direction against the dollar...it would probably be hard for the Japanese to commit themselves to massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they did last autumn" for fear it might not work this time, said Larry Greenberg, international economist at Ried Thunberg in Westport, Conn. \par \par \uc2  In the days and weeks after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen  against both the euro and the dollar was "extremely effective" said Greenberg. That success over the medium term, however, was achieved because \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders' main focus then was on the yen, and not - as now - on the dollar, he added. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last September, often several times on the same global day, both independently and through its counterparts in Europe and the U.S. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped put a stool under the dollar and pulled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from its low at the time of around Y115.84. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Dollar Fire Sale\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But aside from limited pockets of success, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has generally\par fallen very much out of fashion in recent years. There has been some\par coordinated action by central banks - most notably the infamous and\par first ever round of joint Group of Seven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the\par euro on Sept. 22, 2000 - but aside from Japan's lone efforts, the whole\par idea of actively managing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has died down since 1995.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At that time, then-Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin first unveiled the\par strong dollar policy, issuing a closely-watched statement that was\par swiftly followed up with rounds of coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , sending the\par greenback soaring roughly 50% against the yen over the following two\par years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But ironically, while that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sparked a major turning point\par for the dollar, it also effectively marked the end of the U.S. policy of\par guiding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The reason the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was so successful lies largely with the\par fact that it happened at the onset of a major economic expansion in the\par U.S., thus allowing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's actions to produce the textbook\par desired effect: nudge a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the direction it wants to take\par anyway.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now, the situation is markedly different. "Given the backdrop, (in\par which) everyone seems to be fire-selling the U.S. dollar, (the BOJ)\par should just bide its time," and only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if there is a disorderly\par and very sharp plunge of the dollar against the yen, said David Mozina,\par director of fixed income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at Banc of\par America Securities in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is "grossly misaligned" then \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can work as a\par "short-term solution", Mozina said, but the dollar's current malaise is\par broader than that.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar has stumbled to multi-month lows in recent days, hit by a\par cornucopia of factors including doubts about the robustness of the U.S.\par economic rebound, disappointing U.S. corporate profits, a sluggish\par performance by U.S. equity markets and expectations that the current\par account deficit might widen toward 5% of gross domestic product from the\par current 4%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    BOJ Might Still Be Trigger-Happy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some strategists still think that this slide in the dollar heightens\par the chances that the existing chorus of rhetoric of Japanese\par policymakers might give way to action on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets if\par the dollar were to slip to levels somewhere between around Y126 and\par Y120. On Friday in New York, the greenback dipped below Y127 on several\par occasions, and was at Y126.99 in the late afternoon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And despite prevailing expectations that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be futile,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's past preference for the action may make the BOJ more\par trigger-happy than some anticipate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was some speculation to this effect on Friday, after rumors\par emerged in Tokyo that the Bank of Japan had asked Japanese banks if they\par would keep their dealing lines open Monday and that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had advised dealers not to hold short dollar positions over the\par weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par O'Neill's remarks on Wednesday indicate that the U.S. monetary\par authorities "are not going to partake in concerted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " if the\par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Greenberg. But that wouldn't prevent the\par BOJ from acting alone, he cautions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Policy-makers in Japan don't agree with him (O'Neill)," about the\par ineffectiveness of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Greenberg said. "They have always felt\par there can be a net impact in line with what they want", he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Jamie McGeever in London contributed to this report)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020503dy5300cnl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
