\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar firms on Swiss rate cut, BOJ worries. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 928 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2002\par 23:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, May 2 (Reuters) - The dollar closed higher on Thursday after traders shrugged off the latest lukewarm comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill and focused instead on a Swiss rate cut, talk that Japan was concerned about the firm yen and upcoming U.S. payrolls data. \par \par \uc2  Speaking after an address to New York's Japan Society, O'Neill once again tiptoed around the issue of whether the United States continued to support a strong dollar by explaining that fundamentals were far more important to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's direction than speculative forces, which he called a "sideshow." \par \par \uc2   "I do believe that the world evermore has the ability ... to \par \par \uc2   discount a future prospect of relative productivity between \par \par \uc2   economies ... and that's reflected in financial markets," O'Neill said in response to a question on the administration's dollar \par \par \uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Investors Bank and Trust Co. director Tim Mazanec said O'Neill clearly was steering a wide path around the issue. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday he tried to avoid the phrase 'strong dollar' and he pretty much did the same today ... He's saying 'let the markets dictate' (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels). Whether it's the U.S. or \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he directly said verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are ineffective." \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar sank to multi-month lows against a number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after O'Neill made similar comments regarding the strong dollar policy that were viewed by traders as lacking conviction. \par \par \uc2  But on Thursday "O'Neill was old news," said one New York-based trader. He said that as the day progressed, the focus increasingly turned to Friday's U.S. April payrolls report for clues on the pace of the recovery. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters on average expect the data to show payrolls rose 41,000 in April after gaining 58,000 in March. But the unemployment rate is expected to rise to 5.8 percent from 5.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been under pressure recently on fears the economic recovery was not proceeding at a quick enough pace to boost corporate profits, despite the torrid pace of the expansion in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Tom Benfer, vice president at Bank of Montreal in New York, said concerns about the economy and the dollar were overblown. \par \par \uc2  "You still have a huge amount of stimulus in this economy," he said. Economic prospects in Japan and Europe, meanwhile, were less robust, while Germany, Europe's largest economy, was facing labor troubles that could further crimp its recovery. \par \par \uc2  Germany's largest union, IG Metall, on Thursday called 50,000 of its members out for a May 6 strike. \par \par \uc2  EURO \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  SLIP AS SWISS \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  EASES \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained ground against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday after Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised markets with a half-point rate cut. \par \par \uc2  Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was around 1.6095 francs , up 0.35 percent from the previous U.S. close and more than a full centime above seven-month lows at 1.5962 francs set a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank has been concerned about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. But traders said it should not expect the rate cut to continue to depress the franc. \par \par \uc2  "(The SNB) has stopped the bleeding, but it doesn't have much more ammunition," said the New York trader. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains against the Swiss franc helped pull the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to around 90.30 cents, well below Wednesday's peaks at 90.86 cents. \par \par \uc2  A widely expected decision by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave interest rates on hold had little impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH? \par \par \uc2  As O'Neill was disparaging \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a policy tool, traders were on watch for any sign that Japanese authorities might take action to depress the yen after reports overnight that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  had telephoned banks to express its displeasure over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rise. \par \par \uc2  Some traders worried the Bank of Japan might take advantage of thin market conditions during the country's Golden Week holidays to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If there is any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would step in during a holiday, it's (the BOJ)," said Mazanec. But others suspected the bank would stick to verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 127.86 yen, up from 127.47 at the U.S. close on Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 2106GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  calculated from the levels at 4:30 p.m. (2030 GMT) in the \par \par \uc2  previous New York session. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Last