\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar stays on defensive, Swiss franc falls. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2002\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 2 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled to pull off multi-month lows against the euro and the yen on Thursday as doubts over the United States' commitment to a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  persisted. \par \par \uc2  Heavy selling from Japanese investors ahead of the long holiday weekend pushed it to fresh two-month lows below 127 yen early on, but an interest rate cut from the Swiss National Bank helped it bounce off seven-month lows against the Swiss franc and generally helped it off its worst levels. \par \par \uc2  "We have seen a complete sea-change in sentiment towards the dollar, and people are now just looking for excuses to sell," said Neal Kimberley, manager at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  The greenback, already reeling on concerns over the pace of U.S. recovery, suffered one of its biggest one-day falls this year on Wednesday after U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill failed to convince markets over his country's commitment to a strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  Disappointing U.S. manufacturing data only added to pressure on the dollar, reigniting concern that U.S. assets have become overvalued. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose half a percent above 1.61 Swiss francs in the European mid-session after the Swiss National Bank cut interest rates to counter the franc's unwanted strength. \par \par \uc2  This helped the greenback claw back to $0.9050 against the euro but it remained within half a cent of Wednesday's six-month lows. \par \par \uc2  SWISS RATES CUT, ECB AWAITED \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank slashed key short-term interest rates by half a percentage point on Thursday to counter the recent strength of the Swiss franc, which has threatened to undermine a nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "With this step the National Bank is reacting to the rapid appreciation of the Swiss franc against the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has led to an undesirable tightening of monetary conditions in Switzerland," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The move takes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key target range for three-month Libor rates to 0.75-1.75 percentage points, the lowest range since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began targeting Libor in December 2001. \par \par \uc2  "It's a pretty decisive move and not entirely discounted so it will have some negative effect on the Swiss franc," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds its regular policy meeting later in the day, but no change in rates is expected. \par \par \uc2  O'NEILL SPARKS JITTERS \par \par \uc2  Testifying before the Senate Banking Committee on Wednesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill was reluctant to extol the virtues of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though he said there had been no change in the country's commitment to a "strong dollar" policy. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill said he doubted governments could long affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values either through rhetoric or by actual market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing a continuation of dollar selling from yesterday afternoon, with O'Neill's performance giving markets a sense that U.S. commitment to a strong dollar policy is diluted," said Russell Jones, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. could never come out publicly and abandon its strong dollar policy, but behind-the-scenes they could well want the dollar to see an orderly decline." \par \par \uc2  In contrast, Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda reiterated his own mantra on Thursday, saying domestic economic fundamentals did not justify any rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  But his remarks did nothing to lift the dollar with market participants increasingly taking the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at current levels. \par \par \uc2  SLOWING CAPITAL FLOWS THREATEN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  O'Neill said on Wednesday the U.S. economy would be harmed by any moves to narrow its current account deficit, which is hovering around four percent of gross domestic product and is regarded by many as a potential threat to global economic stability. The deficit was $417.429 billion in 2001. \par \par \uc2  As foreign investors in the U.S. grow concerned about the deficit, capital flows into U.S. markets are narrowing. \par \par \uc2  According to recent U.S. Treasury Department data, net inflows of foreign capital into the U.S. totalled around $14.2 billion in January and $15.0 billion in February. That compares with average inflows of $42 billion per month in calendar 2001. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the slowing inflows suggested foreign investors' enthusiasm for buying U.S. assets was drying up because the return on capital had decreased. \par \par \uc2  Concern the U.S. economy is no longer powering a global recovery has already seen the dollar shed three percent against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last month. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020502dy5200ku9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
