\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar pressured by doubts on US recovery, policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2002\par 10:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 2 (Reuters) - The dollar slid to two-month lows against the yen and teetered near six-month lows against the euro on Thursday as concerns over U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and the pace of U.S. recovery continued to weigh. \par \par \uc2  Speaking on Wednesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill was reluctant to extoll the virtues of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though he said there had been no change in the country's commitment to a "strong dollar" policy. \par \par \uc2  Disappointing U.S. manufacturing data only added to pressure on the dollar, reigniting concern that the U.S. recovery was running out of steam. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing a continuation of dollar selling from yesterday afternoon, with O'Neill's performance giving markets a sense that U.S. commitment to a strong dollar policy is diluted," said Russell Jones, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. "The U.S. could never come out publicly and abandon its strong dollar policy, but behind-the-scenes they could well want the dollar to see an orderly decline." \par \par \uc2  Reluctant to go into a long holiday weekend holding dollars, Japanese investors proved eager sellers, knocking the dollar below 127 yen for the first time since early March. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar stood at $0.9060, having recovered slightly from six-month lows set the previous session at $0.9086. \par \par \uc2  Concern the U.S. economy is no longer powering a global recovery has already seen the dollar shed three percent against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last month. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds its regular policy meeting later in the day, but no change in rates is expected. \par \par \uc2  O'NEILL AND KURODA \par \par \uc2  Testifying before the Senate Banking Committee, O'Neill said he doubted governments could long affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values either through rhetoric or by actual market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The market, already growing increasingly bearish about the dollar, interpreted O'Neill's hands-off stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a green light to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing it to new 2002 lows against the euro, Swiss franc and sterling. \par \par \uc2  Joining protests from U.S. manufacturers, American Farm Bureau Federation President Bob Stallman said on Wednesday that U.S. farm exports have been shut out of key international markets due to the high value of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, reiterated his own mantra on Thursday, saying domestic economic fundamentals did not justify any rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  But his remarks did nothing to lift the dollar with market participants increasingly taking the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at current levels. \par \par \uc2  SLOWING CAPITAL FLOWS THREATEN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  O'Neill said on Wednesday the U.S. economy would be harmed by any moves to narrow its current account deficit, which is hovering around four percent of gross domestic product and is regarded by many as a potential threat to global economic stability. \par \par \uc2  The deficit was $417.429 billion in 2001. \par \par \uc2  As foreign investors in the United States grow concerned about the deficit, capital flows into U.S. markets are narrowing. \par \par \uc2  According to recent U.S. Treasury Department data, net inflows of foreign capital into the United States totalled around $14.2 billion in January and $15.0 billion in February. That compares average inflows of $42 billion per month in calendar 2001. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the slowing inflows suggested foreign investors' enthusiasm for buying U.S. assets was drying up because the return on capital had decreased. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020502dy5200go3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
