\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slips to 2-month low vs yen in Tokyo. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 852 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2002\par 08:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 2 (Reuters) - The dollar slid to a two-month low against the yen in Tokyo on Thursday, deepening losses brought on by weaker-than-expected U.S. economic data and bearish comments from Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill. \par \par \uc2  The dollar skidded to a traded low of 126.96 yen before recovering some ground to trade at 127.03/07 by 0230 GMT. Against the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it stood at $0.9068/73 after testing a low of $0.9075. \par \par \uc2  Many traders expect the dollar to soon test this year's low of 126.36 yen set on March 7, and slide even further against the euro as weak-dollar sentiment prevails. \par \par \uc2  "I expect the greenback to slip further during the Tokyo market holidays on Friday and Monday in light of the current bearish mood," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Others also see greater downside risk for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "During (Japan's) holidays, yen can be bought up by foreign institutional investors who wish to reshuffle their portfolios. moving out of U.S. markets and into Japanese equities," said Shuzo Kakuta, senior deputy manager of Mizuho Corporate Bank. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday in New York, the dollar hit two-month lows against the yen at 127.15 yen, six-month lows on the euro at $0.9087 and seven-month lows against the Swiss franc at 1.5968. \par \par \uc2  O'NEILL AND KURODA \par \par \uc2  Testifying before the Senate Banking Committee, O'Neill said he doubted governments could affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values either through rhetoric or by actual market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I'm saying I think there's a real doubt about the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or words about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, said on Thursday he saw no need for the yen to rise since the domestic economy would only bottom out in the second half of the year while the U.S. economy was already on a recovery track. \par \par \uc2  "At this point there is no need for a rise in the yen. We will continue to closely monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said. \par \par \uc2  However, many dealers and analysts say Japanese monetary authorities are unlikely to rush into dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because levels do not yet pose a major threat to corporate profits and because the United States is perceived as not wanting Japan to support the ailing dollar. SLOWING CAPITAL FLOW THREATENS DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  O'Neill said on Wedneday that any moves to narrow the U.S. current account deficit, which is hovering around four percent of gross domestic product and is regarded by many as a potential threat to global economic stability, would harm the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "So I don't find it very appealing to say we're going to cut off our arm because someday we might get a disease in it," he said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. current account deficit was $417.429 billion in 2001. \par \par \uc2  As foreign investors in the United States grow concerned about the deficit, capital flows into U.S. markets are narrowing. \par \par \uc2  According to recent U.S. Treasury Department data, net inflows of foreign capital into the United States totalled around $14.2 billion in January and $15.0 billion in February. \par \par \uc2  That compared to massive fund flows of $509.88 billion for calender 2001 or an average inflow per month of $42 billion. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the slowing inflows suggested foreign investors' enthusiasm in buying U.S. assets was drying up because the return on capital had decreased. \par \par \uc2  Some economists also expect the flight in capital from the United States may end up in the eurozone and Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed that foreign investors bought a net 269.2 billion yen ($2.12 billion) worth of Japanese bonds and stocks in the first three weeks of April, compared with net selling of 402.4 billion yen in March. \par \par \uc2  ($1=127.17 Yen) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0238 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   01 May. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9068   0.9062   +0.07    +1.84   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        126.92   127.47   -0.43    -3.58   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    115.19   115.45   -0.23    -1.75   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.6016   1.6034   -0.11    -3.48   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.4669   1.4648   +0.14    +0.88   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5603   1.5593   +0.06    -1.97   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5391   0.5396   -0.09    +5.58   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4528   1.4516   +0.08    -1.71   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6182   0.6184   -0.03    +0.96   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ..  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \pa