\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr clings to gains after consumer confidence dips. \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2002\par 16:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  1410 GMT - Dollar struggles to hold modest gains against key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after consumer confidence slips in April, but slightly better than market's expectations. \par \par \uc2  - Though stocks accelerate early rally, sentiment still favors further dollar selling as investors scale back on expectations of U.S. economic rebound. \par \par \uc2  - Consumer Confidence for April comes in at 108.8 from upwardly-revised 110.7 in prior month. Analysts polled by Reuters had expected a 108.0 reading. But present situation and expectations gauges also fell, reflecting consumers' continuing near-term pessimism about the economy. \par \par \uc2  - Chicago Purchasing Managers Index slips to 54.7 in April vs. 55.7 in prior month. Prices paid accelerate to 55.4 versus 51.2 in March. \par \par \uc2  - Euro/dollar falls back from NY session highs, trades near 90.15 cents. \par \par \uc2  - Euro dips after German IG Metall engineering union overwhelmingly votes to strike amid stalled wage negotiations. Though German and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers have in recent days sought to downplay the effects of a strike on the euro zone's largest economy, analysts say it could hamper what is already perceived as a modest recovery. \par \par \uc2  - Dollar/yen gyrates around 128.30 level , off session peaks above 128.50 but above 1-1/2 month lows at 127.65 set a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  - Versus yen, euro buys 115.60 yen, virtually flat on the day . \par \par \uc2  - Yen tempered by Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi, who said yen appreciation was inappropriate. However, traders doubtful that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at levels above 125. \par \par \uc2  - A Reuters poll of 13 U.S. asset allocators showed managers scaling back on U.S. equities, to 48 percent from 49 percent in the previous month. Same managers modestly added to holdings of euro zone assets. Highlights greenback's central quandary over whether capital flows will be sufficent to offset massive U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  - Stocks apparently cheer consumer data, after Monday marked longest losing streak in 19 months. Dow Jones Industrial Average shoots to session highs around 80 points, Nasdaq Technology average gains 16 points. \par \par \uc2  For full report on major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020430dy4u00sq1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
