\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips as markets question US recovery. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2002\par 12:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 29 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled to its lowest for nearly four months against the euro and set a 1-1/2 month trough on the yen on Monday as poor U.S. data last week and anticipation of more to come heightened U.S. recovery concerns. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest upset began last week and was compounded on Friday by a disappointing U.S. consumer sentiment report which clouded prospects for a strong recovery. \par \par \uc2  Weak quarterly earnings and dreary corporate forecasts dragged U.S. stocks down on Friday to their worst weekly performance since the week after September 11. \par \par \uc2  "The market is entering into a dollar bearish mode and the dollar's outlook remains questionable," Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. assets are less appealing now than 1-1/2 years ago partly because of poor performance of U.S. equities. And here you start to have a problem because you need a fair amount of capital inflows to fund the large current account deficit." \par \par \uc2  In thin trading with financial markets in Japan closed for the Green Day holiday, the dollar fell as low as 127.65, losing nearly three yen in just a week. \par \par \uc2  The greenback also touched $0.9045 against the euro and 5-1/2 month lows on the Swiss franc and sterling. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the greenback fell to its lowest level since December on the FINEX trade-weighted index illustrating the dollar's broad-based weakness. \par \par \uc2  This week's key U.S. data include consumer confidence data on Tuesday, the Institute for Supply Management (ISM) survey on Wednesday, followed by labour market data and services sector survey on Friday. \par \par \uc2  SENATE HEARING, ECB \par \par \uc2  Analysts polled by Reuters expect Tuesday's consumer confidence index to fall to 107.0 in April from 110.2 in March. The ISM index (formerly NAPM) is forecast to dip to 55.4 in April from 55.6. The jobs data is seen a little better in comparison, with the unemployment rate expected to remain unchanged at 5.7 percent in April. \par \par \uc2  "This week's data will hardly support the dollar," said Armin Mekelburg, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers are attributing the dollar's weakness to nervousness ahead of a closely-watched U.S. Senate banking committee hearing on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  This hearing has potential to become a stormy event with members of Congress due to hear from Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill, defender of the U.S. strong dollar policy, and industry groups irritated at the dollar's strength. \par \par \uc2  The committee will hear from Jerry Jasinowski, president of the National Association of Manufacturers and Fred Bergsten, Director at Institute of International Economics. \par \par \uc2  Dollar strength has been a thorn in the side of American manufacturers, who have made loud and frequent complaints to the Treasury Department, the White House and Congress. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yen was backed by strong performance of Japanese equities in recent sessions, compared with Wall Street where profit worries have kept stocks depressed. \par \par \uc2  But the Japanese monetary authorities are likely to step up their verbal campaign against a stronger yen although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still more than 10 yen off the level \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar/yen goes below 120 the chance of outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is high," Fornasari said. \par \par \uc2  For the euro, the focus is on the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's council meeting on Thursday. Analysts expect the ECB to leave rates unchanged at 3.25 percent with recent euro zone data showing inflation falling against a backdrop of moderate growth. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone current account data showed net portfolio investment outflow from the region of 9.9 billion euros in February, compared with an outflow of 41.3 billion in January. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020429dy4t00lm9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
