\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar stabilises ahead of U.S. GDP data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2002\par 13:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 26 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied above recent lows against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday as dealers grew nervous of extending its losses ahead of key U.S. economic data. \par \par \uc2  Concern the U.S. economy is no longer powering a global recovery has weighed heavily on the greenback this week, encouraging investors to diversify out of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Traders pushed the dollar to three-month lows against the euro in early trade, but failed to break the psychological $0.90 level, allowing the dollar to bounce back into positive territory. \par \par \uc2  "The market is reluctant to push the dollar through further technical levels until they see this afternoon's U.S. data," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "Uncertainty over the pace of U.S. recovery has already sent the dollar substantially lower this week, and the market is now taking a pause." \par \par \uc2  The dollar also managed to hold above Thursday's six-week lows of 128.14 yen, and was trading at 128.39/43 at 1140 GMT, a quarter percent loss from the U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  Growth data at 1230 GMT is expected to show the U.S. economy grew by a robust annualised 4.8 percent in the first quarter, helped by massive restocking of inventories. \par \par \uc2  However, disappointing releases in recent days have raised concern that this heady pace of growth will not be sustained in the second quarter, making U.S. equities look overvalued. \par \par \uc2  "People know the first quarter is likely to be strong, but they are concerned about growth potential for the rest of the year," said Sonja Hellemann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock index futures were indicating a stronger open on Wall Street, after markets closed virtually unchanged on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was also looking weaker, trading close to key chart support against the dollar at $1.45, after data showing Britain's economy grew by only 0.1 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  SWISS FRANC SETBACK \par \par \uc2  The dollar bounced back by a centime from four-month lows below 1.6270 Swiss francs set early in European trade, helped by wariness the Swiss National Bank could take action to stem its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unwanted strength. \par \par \uc2  "There were rumours the SNB was bidding, and this helped dollar/Swiss higher," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc's firmness has prompted increasingly loud complaints from the country's exporters whose products have become dearer abroad, as well as the tourist industry. \par \par \uc2  To counter franc strength, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut its money market repo rate and offered generous money market liquidity. It has also explicitly left open the possibility of cutting official interest rates if its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  faced renewed upward pressure. \par \par \uc2  Swiss National Bank President Jean-Pierre Roth said on Friday a weaker Swiss franc could jeopardise price stability and have only short-term economic benefits. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank had no comment Friday on market talk that it was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "We never comment on market rumours," an SNB official said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by 25 basis points as expected, but the Swedish crown shrugged off the news, still reeling from losses announced earlier this week by key Swedish stock Ericsson. \par \par \uc2  The crown weakened to two-month lows against the euro of 9.255. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN OFFICIALS SPEAK \par \par \uc2  The yen retained a firm bias, with Japanese investors reluctant to hold long dollar positions ahead of next week's Golden Week holidays. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials continued to lament the yen's unwanted strength in Tokyo trading hours. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said he was watching exchange rate movements with "great interest", while senior finance ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi repeated his mantra that there were no new factors warranting the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda broke a short silence but only to state that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has not changed. \par \par \uc2  That sounded surprisingly timid to dealers who had feared he would escalate the warnings to: "we stand ready to act in the markets", a phrase which has preceded \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's reticence fed suspicions that the risk of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is limited right now. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real effective exchange rate measures the yen against 15 major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that shows an average for April so far of 119.4, still eight percent lower than the 130.00 level held in September when it last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020426dy4q00nxs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
