\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar under seige, MOF proves a reluctant ally. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 977 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2002\par 10:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 26 (Reuters) - The dollar was under siege in Asian trading on Friday with Japanese officials making only vague noises about support while formerly loyal investors fretted over the uncertain pace of U.S. recovery. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trade the dollar was being pressed at 128.60 yen, having bounced from 128.15 lows offshore only to meet fresh offers from Japanese exporters and a Swiss name at 129.92. \par \par \uc2  It has now retreated four percent in as many weeks with more than a few investment funds forced to cut long positions built up in the expectation of yen weakness toward 140.00 - a view prevalant early in the year. \par \par \uc2  The technical picture has also soured with the loss of 129.20 support on Thursday. Analysts pegged immediate chart support at 128.15, followed by 127.75 and 127.30 but would not be surprised to see the dollar recoil all the way to the year lows at 126.36. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was in just as much trouble elsewhere, with the euro holding at $0.8975 after hitting a three-month peak at $0.8993 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported strong resistance at 89 cents, including an options barrier, but also felt a break of this level would attract speculative and momentum buyers. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could gain a reprieve should U.S. GDP figures due later Friday prove even stronger than expected. Average forecasts are that GDP grew a remarkable 4.8 percent annualised in Q1, helped by massive re-stocking of inventories. \par \par \uc2  However, to some extent this is old news and recent figures have suggested Q2 will be much slower while corporate earnings are showing few signs of a sustained recovery. \par \par \uc2  That in turn makes Wall street less attractive and, while foreign appetite for U.S. debt still seems healthy, any faltering in offshore flows can take a toll on the dollar given the U.S. has to find over $30 billion a week to fund its trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  NO ESCALATION, YET \par \par \uc2  The dollar's losses are the yen's gains, but these are not welcomed by Japanese officials who have the idea that a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help the economy through higher exports. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa was the first to complain today, saying he was watching exchange rate movements with "great interest." \par \par \uc2  Senior MOF official Zembei Mizoguchi chimed in, repeating his mantra that there were no new factors warranting the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda broke his short silence but only to state that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has not changed. \par \par \uc2  That sounded surprisingly timid to dealers who had feared he would escalate the warnings to: "we stand ready to act in the markets", a phrase which has preceded \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's reticence thus fed suspicions that the risk of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is limited right now. \par \par \uc2  One restraining influence could be that this was not a case of yen strength but dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's real effective exchange rate measures the yen against 15 major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that shows an average for April so far of 119.4, still eight percent lower than the 130.00 level held in September when it last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar's Finex trade weighted index had slumped to its lowest level this year, though it was still only a relatively modest 4.7 percent below the 15-year highs it set last July. \par \par \uc2  The MOF could also be wary of upsetting Washington what with the powerful Senate Banking Committee due to discuss the strong-dollar policy next week and whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars back in September was a manipulation of exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  "All this suggests it's hard to imagine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would emerge at the 128 yen level," a dealer at a major Japanese city bank said. "Besides, with Japanese stocks resilient lately, I think authorities would bear the yen's rise to some extent." \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei was about one percent lower on Friday, but is still nine percent higher on the year so far while the S&P 500 is five percent lower. \par \par \uc2  There have also been a few tentative signs that the Japanese economy could be turning a corner. The trade surplus has risen strongly in the last two months while data Friday showed a surprise dip in the unemployment rate to 5.2 percent in March. \par \par \uc2  "The main message from the data is that it looks like the Bank of Japan will have no reason to consider any further monetary easing in the next few months," said Chris Walker, an economist at CSFB. \par \par \uc2  And that was one danger less for the yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0558 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   25 Apr. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- Euro/dlr \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  0.8975   0.8976   -0.01    +0.80   0.8904 Dlr/yen        128.59\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    128.70   -0.09    -2.31   131.63 Euro/yen    115.44   115.49\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -0.04    -1.54   117.24 Dlr/swiss      1.630