\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Falls Against Yen, Euro Despite Strong U.S. GDP Data \b0\par\par\uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2002\par 01:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Capping a week of broad-based losses, the dollar continued to retreat against its major counterparts Friday, breaking below key levels against the euro and the yen and hitting fresh lows for the year against the pound and the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Most of the retrenchment took place after midmorning Friday in New York amid light trading volume, when first the dollar hit a fresh six-week trough against the yen, slipping below 128 yen for the first time since mid-March. Next, the euro broke above the key 90 cents threshold, marking the first time the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has traded above 90 cents since the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  "The problem with the dollar this whole past week has been concern that the U.S. economic recovery is not going to be as strong as expected," said Mary Ann Hurley, senior Treasurys trader in Seattle at brokerage D.A. Davidson. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the dollar's declines came despite a strong reading of economic activity for the first quarter. An advance report on U.S. gross domestic product data for the first quarter, showed growth of 5.8%, a full percentage point higher than economists' expectations. \par \par \uc2  But that might indicate the initial bounce in industrial activity in the first quarter may not continue to rise as strongly later in the year, Ms. Hurley said, a factor that has fed a wave dollar selling all week. The dollar's battering this week has come largely on the back of a sense that while the U.S. may not have experienced much of a recession, it also won't see much of a recovery, prompting investors to switch their funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher-yielding returns. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in New York, the dollar was at 127.80 yen, its intraday low and down from 128.63 yen late Thursday. The euro was at $0.9020, also its intraday high against the dollar and down from around $0.8980. Sterling was changing hands at $1.4570, up from $1.4551. \par \par \uc2  There was some skepticism among U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists as to whether the dollar will remain under as much pressure next week, however. \par \par \uc2  Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with MG Financial Group in New York noted that on each of the last three occasions in which the euro has breached its 200-day moving average against the dollar, it has rallied by an average of 2% before falling back again. Since its inception in January 1999, the euro has repeatedly failed to sustain major rallies against the dollar, which has prompted a certain level of frustration for many investors. \par \par \uc2  There are a number of negative factors that may weigh on the euro over the coming weeks, including the threat of strike actions in Germany -- the euro zone's biggest economy -- by that country's most influential trade union, IG Metall, in a wage dispute, Mr. Laidi noted. \par \par \uc2  But a convincing move above 90 cents could help attract more investors into this rally, analysts think. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the advance of the yen might also turn tail, some said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has gained over 2% on the dollar this week, may be currently well bid because of fairly transient technical factors as well as the flow of funds to Japan, which may no longer support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the governing Liberal Democratic Party performs badly in by-elections on Sunday, said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers in New York. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who reached his first anniversary in office Friday, has failed to make much headway in planned reforms of the ailing domestic banking system. The financial sector's huge mountain of bad debts and potentially nonperforming loans, estimated to be in the region of 150 trillion yen. and the already dramatic erosion of Mr. Koizumi's public approval ratings "may well be another time bomb out there" that can hurt the yen, said Ms. Rhame. \par \par \uc2  The possibility that the Japanese finance ministry might authorize \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen could also keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening further, some traders think. \par \par \uc2  Although the yen did break decisively below the important 128 yen level -- an area where strategists warn the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to weaken the yen -- there is some debate however, as to whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be prepared to take any action before the dollar falls toYEN 125. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese finance ministry -- which is responsible for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy -- has made no secret of the fact that it would prefer a weaker yen, which helps support exports and lift the country's stagnant economy, but traders noted that the ministry rarely authorizes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these kind of levels. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also be swimming against a tide of general dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  The dollar did bounce back a bit against the Swiss franc in early trading after Swiss National Bank President Jean-Pierre Roth said the bank will continue to watch \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and react should an adverse situation occur. The SNB has made no secret of its opposition to a strong franc. \par \par \uc2  He also reiterated however that any strategy to weaken the franc would backfire because it would result in a loosening of monetary policy and endanger price stability. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021030dy4q006si\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
