\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar digs five-week lows on yen, MOF gripes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2002\par 05:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 25 (Reuters) - The dollar dug to five-week lows on the yen on Thursday but managed to keep losses to a minimum after verbal support from Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was limp across the board as U.S. data fed talk the disappointing pace of an economic recovery had investors diversifying out of safe-haven U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve's anecdotal "beige book" report on Wednesday said the U.S. economy was improving, but cited concerns about the speed and consistency of an upturn. Soft durable goods data and another weak performance by Wall Street also weighed on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Still, the dollar was not without help from the Japanese authorities, ever keen to cap a yen rise to make sure exports steered the country out of recession. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said he did not see the yen strengthening further against the dollar because economic fundamentals were stronger in the United States. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dipped to lows of 129.31 yen before clawing back to 129.50 by midday, not far from late New York levels of 129.55. It managed to stave off a break of key retracement support at 129.20 and stop-loss orders at 129.00. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's under pressure, but a lack of positive factors for the yen means the range will remain tight," a trader at a Japanese trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was buoyant on the dollar, but large option expiries at $0.8900 and $0.8950 later in the day kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a range. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted at $0.8921 , off three-month peaks of $0.8932 in New York, while Japanese exporter sales knocked it lower on the yen at 115.50 from 115.68. \par \par \uc2  The market looked ahead to Germany's Ifo business sentiment survey due later on Thursday which was expected to show a modest rise to 92.1 from 91.8. \par \par \uc2  MUSING ON FLOWS \par \par \uc2  Talk swirled that Japanese investors would begin sending more money abroad soon, though many were sceptical and suspected outflows would be modest at best as banks and insurers shied from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. \par \par \uc2  Official data on Thursday showed Japanese investors bought a net 96.0 billion yen ($740.5 million) worth of foreign bonds last week and a net 98.6 billion yen in foreign stocks, but the figures proved much less than what the market had speculated. \par \par \uc2  Some were equally jaded by talk that foreign funds were raising their weightings in Japanese shares on some signs the economy had hit a bottom. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought a hefty 166.4 billion yen in shares last week, but the data also showed them selling a net 121.6 billion yen in Japanese bonds. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign investors may be raising their share of Japanese stocks, but it'll be limited to some portfolio adjustments that won't see them buying aggressively in any way," said Masayuki Yamamoto, research analyst at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "Economic growth in Japan will only be a result of a U.S. recovery, and in that case, the U.S. will still be the better place to invest in," he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders also had second thoughts about Japanese authorities' recent complaints about a stronger yen, as many suspected the risk of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was limited unless the dollar fell toward 125.00. \par \par \uc2  They also note \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be politically sensitive for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the Senate Banking Committee will meet next week to discuss the strong-dollar policy and its impact on U.S. industry. ($1=129.62 Yen). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0329 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 24 Apr.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.8920   0.8926   -0.07    +0.18   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen               129.51   129.55   -0.03    -1.61   131.63\par  Euro/yen              115.55   115.68   -0.11    -1.44   117.24\par  Dlr/swiss             1.6425   1.6418   +0.04    -1.02   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr               1.4493   1.4492   +0.01    -0.33   1.4541\par  Can/dlr               1.5665   1.5673   -0.05    -1.58   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr               0.5435   0.5434   +0.02    +6.44   0.5106\par  Euro/swiss            1.4658   1.4657   +0.01    -0.83   1.4780\par  Euro/stg              0.6155   0.6158   -0.05    +0.52   0.6123.\par \par \par \par     For full report on major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ..        Debt...      Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Official rates...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary