\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar edges ahead as MOF talks down yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2002\par 09:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 24 (Reuters) - The dollar kept an edge against the yen on Wednesday as Tokyo resumed its usual griping over a stronger Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Having turned unusually timid early in the week, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  rediscovered its voice late on Tuesday when its head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Haruhiko Kuroda, told journalists in New York that the yen should not appreciate against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  He was backed up on Wednesday by senior MOF official Zembei Mizoguchi who argued that there were no new factors to prompt the yen to strengthen. \par \par \uc2  The market duly nudged the dollar higher, but only reached 130.44 yen from 130.20 in New York before Japanese exporter offers blocked the way. \par \par \uc2  It hovered at 130.30 yen by late Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's been well supported, but Japanese exporters are quite keen to sell the dollar ahead of the long holiday," a trader at a Japanese bank said, referring to Japan's "Golden Week" holidays beginning next Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was expected to hold its ground ahead of a report from rating agency Moody's on its review of Japan's sovereign rating, due within the next few weeks and expected to include a downgrade of at least one notch. \par \par \uc2  The euro was equally sluggish, sitting at 115.71 yen from 115.69 in New York. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quiet on the dollar at $0.8878 after finishing little changed at $0.8885 in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  RESILIENT EURO \par \par \uc2  While there is much talk that looming strikes in Germany and the shock success of far-right nationalist Jean-Marie Le Pen in France should be negative for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro has proved resilient in recent days. \par \par \uc2  One or more of the euro-zone national central banks was said to be buying euros, apparently for commercial purposes, around $0.8850. \par \par \uc2  "It's quite bizarre the way the euro's been holding up given all the negative news out of Europe," noted Marshall Gittler, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "I have to assume people really are uncertain about the U.S. recovery and whether it can keep attracting enough offshore money to fund its current account deficit," he added. \par \par \uc2  The deficit has long been shrugged off by U.S. officials and largely ignored by markets, but now that it is steadily nearing five percent of gross domestic product, more doomsayers are appearing in print. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, Nobel Prize-winning economist Robert Mundell said the deficit was driving the nation deeper into the debt of foreign creditors and could undermine the strength of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sudden reversion to verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  left traders exasperated since only a day ago Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa had insisted that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be determined by markets. \par \par \uc2  "When they say \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left to the market, what they mean is, 'left to the market as long as the market does what we want it to do'," said Bank of America's Gittler. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers were hoping that U.S. durable goods orders and the Federal Reserve's Beige Book due out later on Wednesday might give them something to trade on. \par \par \uc2  However, Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan made it abundantly clear last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had doubts about the pace of recovery and was in no rush to raise interest rates, thus greatly limiting the potential impact of any single piece of data. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0613 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   23 Apr. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.8883   0.8885   -0.02    -0.24   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        130.25   130.20   +0.04    -1.05   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    115.80   115.69   +0.10    -1.23   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.6500   1.6498   +0.01    -0.57   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.4474   1.4467   +0.05    -0.46   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5717   1.5724   -0.04    -1.26   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5386   0.5383   +0.06    +5.48   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4659   1.4661   -0.01    -0.82   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6138   0.6139   -0.02    +0.24   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ..  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Official rates...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......   Top events........\par \f28 \par \par \