\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Dips Below Y130, But No Sign Of BOJ Action \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Hardy \par \uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2002\par 17:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Following a disappointing U.S. jobless claims number Thursday, dollar/yen tumbled quickly through the Bank of Japan's 'line in the sand' at Y130, but so far no reaction from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is apparent. \par \par \uc2  But dealers say that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be inevitable, if the pair fails to remain above the key level. Analysts widely consider Y130 to the dollar to be strongest level acceptable for Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  At about 11.00 a.m. EDT (1500 GMT), the dollar was at Y129.90, compared with an intraday low of Y129.76 earlier in New York, Y130.55 in London and Y130.73 late in New York. \par \par \uc2  Overnight Thursday, Haruhiko Kuroda, administrative vice finance minister for international affairs had again reiterated the frequently voiced hostility of the Japanese authorities to a firming yen. \par \par \uc2  "There are no grounds for the yen to strengthen," he said,  "although Japan's economy is likely to post growth in the second half of the year, it's still behind the U.S. and Europe." \par \par \uc2  And if the BOJ intends to defend the Y130 level, it has had plenty of time to get ready. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen saw its April high at Y133.81 on the first day of the month, and since then, the yen has been gaining on the dollar virtually every day. \par \par \uc2  This week the pace of decline has actually picked up, with the dollar dropping more than two yen to Thursday's low at Y129.76. \par \par \uc2  According to analysts, further yen gains may well be in the cards. \par \par \uc2  "I think we'll push right down - to below Y129 - Kuroda has come out two nights in a row and to no effect," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston adding, "we see real money going into the country." \par \par \uc2  Ku Shin, a global fund manager at Banc One Investment Advisors in Columbus, Ohio, said he does not expect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaches Y127. Until that point, Shin said he would expect "rhetoric" from Japanese policymakers in an effort to rein back the yen's advance. \par \par \uc2  Mazanec also believes that the BOJ will ultimately take action if the yen doesn't back off soon. \par \par \uc2  "We have a chance of getting below Y129 - and then they'll come in - and maybe we'll get bounce back," he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Hardy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2122;\par john.hardy@dowjones.com (John Parry contributed to this report).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020418dy4i005v8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
