\par\par \b \uc2  Yen clings to gains as MOF stays mum. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2002\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 17 (Reuters) - The yen held a grip on its gains on Wednesday, taking heart in an absence of comments from Japanese authorities to stem a rise. \par \par \uc2  Traders had braced for verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, remembering how loudly it protested against an export-damaging rise in the yen last week when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened to break the 130.00 level. \par \par \uc2  But as officials stayed mum for the day, yen bulls suspected Japan might be constrained by the proximity of the Group of Seven meeting this weekend. \par \par \uc2  That pinned the dollar at 131.05 yen by late Asian trade from early highs at 131.31 and 131.16 late in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar did manage to fight off early lows of 130.90 at one point by talk of some buying from Japanese public funds. \par \par \uc2  Still, the break of 131.30 support offshore has soured the technical picture and analysts looked for a pullback to support at 130.40/60, if not a test of last week's three-week lows around 130.20. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also on the defensive at 115.70 yen after dipping as deep as 115.35 offshore, with analysts seeing risks of a pullback to 115.00 if not 114.65 support. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fared slightly better on the dollar. It stood at $0.8830 from $0.8824 late in New York, though traders reported stiff resistance around $0.8840. \par \par \uc2  The euro got a brief lift offshore when wild rumours circulated that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan had resigned or even that he had suffered a heart attack. \par \par \uc2  Neither was the case and he was set to testify on the economy later Wednesday with analysts generally assuming he will stick with a cautiously optimistic line. \par \par \uc2  That could strengthen market hopes that the Fed was in no rush to tighten monetary policy and thus support equity and bond prices. \par \par \uc2  "We're expecting market-friendly comments from Greenspan, and if U.S. stocks and bonds react accordingly, we could see some effect on the dollar as well," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street is already on a roll, with the Dow jumping two percent on Tuesday on upbeat earnings news while favourable results from Intel after the bell helped Nasdaq futures rise another one percent. \par \par \uc2  ONUS ON MOF \par \par \uc2  But even those equity gains failed to lift the dollar, proving the market was still very long of the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The yen's resilience in the face of Standard and Poor's downgrade of Japan on Monday suggested the market was equally short of yen, having bet large that the advent of the new fiscal year in Japan would see a flood of money offshore. \par \par \uc2  In the event, fund flows have been sporadic at best with many institutions reluctant to take \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk or simply too weak financially to chance foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  "It's really starting to sink in that the Japanese flows abroad this year won't be very big," said a trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  "Instead, there's talk that foreign investors may be buying into Japanese stocks on hopes for a cyclical economic recovery and that would support the yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese exporters have also been buying yen forward, taking advantage of its weakness to lock in favourable exchange rates months in advance. \par \par \uc2  That would be problematic for any intention by the MOF to restrain the yen with officials' recent repeat of threats that Japan's fundamentals did not warrant a higher yen. \par \par \uc2  But some wondered if the balance would prove tough ahead of the G7 where Japanese officials were expected to express hope the economy was finally crawling out of recession. \par \par \uc2  Both White House economist Glen Hubbard and U.S. Trade Representative Robert Zoellick had made a point recently of warning Japan that it could not rely on a weaker yen and exports to pull itself out of trouble. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0621 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   16 Apr. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.8832   0.8824   +0.09    -0.81   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen        131.04   131.16   -0.09    -0.45   131.63\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    115.69   115.74   -0.04    -1.32   117.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.6631   1.6637   -0.04    +0.22   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr        1.4413   1.4401   +0.08    -0.88   1.4541\par  Can/dlr        1.5815   1.5817   -0.01    -0.64   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr        0.5336   0.5332   +0.08    +4.50   0.5106\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4691   1.4679   +0.08    -0.60   1.4780\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/stg    0.6125   0.6122   +0.05    +0.03   0.6123\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ..  Debt... Indicators...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par 