\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa Gives Encouragement To Worried Dollar Bulls \b0\par\par\uc2 811 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2002\par 23:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By John Hardy \par \par \uc2  Of Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Dollar bulls got some encouragement from comments Wednesday which expressed  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's concern over the yen's recent burst of strength. \par \par \uc2  When asked by journalists in Tokyo if the dollar would fall below Y130.00, Shiokawa forthrightly replied, "It probably won't." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts outside Japan cautioned investors not to read too much into Shiokawa's comments, but also noted that the large number of traders who are anxiously awaiting a sign that a new rally in dollar/yen is underway must be heartened by his apparent determination to discourage dollar/yen selling. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers take Shiokawa's observations very seriously, even when he isn't voicing his ministry's official position. \par \par \uc2  "It shows where Japanese comfort levels are," said Larry Greenberg, international economist at Ried, Thunberg in Westport CT, "I would interpret it that he means that the dollar - over the year - will average above Y130." \par \par \uc2  The dollar-supportive spirit of the comments was welcome in a rattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which is still recovering from its experience a month ago when dollar/yen plummeted in ten days to three-month lows at Y126.53 from highs around Y135, falling far further and more quickly than analysts had expected. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around Y130.40 when Shiokawa's comments hit the wires and many in the market saw them as designed to influence current trading levels, possibly suggesting that Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the cards. The dollar spiked to an intraday high at Y130.99 on the finance minister's remarks, but fell back again as global trading progressed. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the dollar was at Y130.69, up from Y130.44 earlier in London and close to Y131.01 in late New York trading Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "I think (Shiokawa's comment) is pretty significant," said Chris Melendez, president of Tempest Asset Management in Irvine, CA, "and it confirms the range he quoted a couple of months ago." \par \par \uc2  And Shiokawa is clearly a firm believer in a strong dollar/yen, saying earlier this month, "I don't think Y135 (to the dollar) is unacceptable." \par \par \uc2  At that time, in related comments, he was even more specific. After noting at that time - early April - dollar/yen "is stable at Y133 to Y132," Shiokawa added, "I think this is as the market should be." \par \par \uc2  It's therefore not surprising that speculative position-takers - like Melendez - believe that Shiokawa will use all his influence to try to ensure that dollar/yen continues to trade in a Y130-Y135 range. \par \par \uc2  However, other analysts downplayed the significance of Shiokawa's comments. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the policymaker was suggesting anything new," said Hiroshi Morikawa, senior economist at UFJ Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa has said this before - but I don't think he expressed the view of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the Treasury - I don't think any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come (as a result of) his comments," said Morikawa. \par \par \uc2  Like many others, Morikawa holds the view that the yen's recent strength is largely a result of the market's disappointment at the failure of Japanese financial institutions to start pushing funds abroad at the beginning of the new Japanese financial year. \par \par \uc2  This in turn he suggests, has led dealers to cut long dollar/yen positions - which no longer look to be the sure route to profits they were hoping for. \par \par \uc2  "Before the beginning of April, everybody expected big investment outflows from the life insurance companies - but so far we haven't seen any movement at all," Morikawa said. \par \par \uc2  However, other analysts take a middle course in assessing the significance of Shiokawa's jawboning, seeing his comments as aimed primarily at establishing a long-term "line in the sand" for dollar/yen, rather than raising the specter of near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  And with this view, Reid, Thunberg's Greenberg doesn't expect any near-term BOJ reaction, even if dollar/yen falls further. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't mean that (the dollar) won't go below Y130 - it's just that (Shiokawa) wouldn't expect it to stay there," he said, "you're betting against the house if you budget dollar/yen below Y130." \par \par \uc2  And Greenberg also points out that this is not a good time for anyone to judge the yen's real market strength, with traders currently paying scant attention to economic fundamentals, distracted by volatility generated by the turmoil in the Mideast. \par \par \uc2  "At anything between the low Y130s and high Y129s, (dollar/yen) is very good value," said Tempest's Melendez, adding that his clients believe that dollar/yen buys at the bottom end of the Y130-Y135 range probably now face reduced risk of loss. \par \par \uc2  -By John Hardy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2122; john.hardy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020410dy4a00ct5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
