\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen falls back after Japan gives warning. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 14:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 11 (Reuters) - The yen retreated further on Monday after last week's breakneck rally, weighed down by poor data and more signals from Japan that it could act to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  The yen shed around a third of a percent against the dollar and the euro, while the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady on the day after failing to maintain last week's gains against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international bureau, Zembei Mizoguchi, said Japan was ready to take steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary, adding that rapid moves were not desirable. Meanwhile, a key gauge of Japanese corporate capital spending plummeted in January. \par \par \uc2  "The comments are the first line of defence. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if we got to 120 (per dollar)," said Rob Hayward, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-Amro. \par \par \uc2  "If we don't move to test 130 in the next 24 hours, we could retest the downside to test the authorities' resolve." \par \par \uc2  At 1240 GMT, the dollar was trading off the session's highs at 128.80 yen, about 2.5 yen above last week's lows but still below the 130-135 range it had held from late December until last week's surge on the back of Japanese stock market gains. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading around 112.50 per euro, more than a yen down from last week's three-month highs. \par \par \uc2  The euro was rangebound against the dollar, trading around $0.8740. \par \par \uc2  TRADE SPAT \par \par \uc2  Analysts were also keeping a close eye on the ongoing dispute between the U.S. and many of its trading partners after Washington's decision to impose tariffs on steel imports. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Commerce Undersecretary Grant Aldonas told the Financial Times in an interview that other sectors could also face tariffs unless Europe and Japan reflate their economies. \par \par \uc2  "People will start to wonder how much they remain behind a strong dollar," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  The euro nevertheless has failed to defend last week's 1-1/2 month high around $0.8840, after the greenback got support from stronger than expected U.S. jobs data on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "This confirms our view that the market has not changed its view on the euro's fundamentals and consequently the upward potential in euro/dollar is very limited," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at WestLB in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the world's top central bankers meeting in Switzerland said they were more optimistic about the global recovery than they were two months ago. \par \par \uc2  Bank of England Governor Sir Edward George briefing reporters after chairing a G-10 meeting of central bankers said there were signs the global economy was on the mend though it was too early to say whether interest rates had bottomed out. \par \par \uc2  MARKETS WARY OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Yen bulls were disappointed that Tokyo's Nikkei stock average could not sustain an early push above 12,000. It ended the day down 33.51 points or 0.28 percent at 11,919.30. \par \par \uc2  The sharp rise in Tokyo stocks in the last couple of weeks was a principal driver behind the yen's sharp rally last week, with stock market data showing overseas investors bought shares to the tune of a net 113.87 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  In a continued display of Japanese government displeasure over the yen's speedy rise and its potential to erode export competitiveness, the MOF's Mizoguchi said, "We always have readiness to deal with various situations." \par \par \uc2  Dealers interpreted Mizoguchi's comments to mean the MOF was not content with the dollar's bounce from last week's near-three month low of 126.36 yen and wanted to see it higher. \par \par \uc2  "Comments have continued from Japanese officials which seem to indicate they would like to see the 130-135 dollar/yen trading range become reinstated again," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  Japan's core private-sector machinery orders, a key gauge of corporate capital spending, fell a worse-than-expected 15.6 percent in January from the previous month. \par \par \uc2  Largely inured to gloomy Japanese data, analysts noted this figure should be a leading indicator of investment, suggesting the economy will get worse before it gets better. \par \par \uc2  "On the back of that data it is understandable the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not want to see the yen appreciate any more. You would see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it did," said Jesper Dannesboe, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also getting no favours from the whiff of a new corruption scandal hitting Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party which could further undermine public support for Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi and his bid for structural reform. \par \par \uc2  A former top aide of one of the LDP's key reformers was arrested on Friday for allegedly evading income taxes. \par \par \uc2  ASSET ALLOCATION \par \par \uc2  Traders also waited for news from Japanese institutional investors, who are mapping out investment plans for the new business year. \par \par \uc2  A council under the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare is set to announce its asset allocation plan late on Tuesday for the year starting on April 1. \par \par \uc2  Fresh funds to be allocated for next fiscal year are seen at around nine trillion yen and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is keen to find out what portion might be shifted to foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  At the end of last March, the Fund held about 1.06 trillion yen or 4.08 percent of its assets in foreign bonds. Total assets amounted to 27.5 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020311dy3b00qeh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
