\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen suffers setback after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  warning. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 11 (Reuters) - The yen surrendered further ground on Monday following last week's furious rally after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down and more signs emerged that the country remains firmly in recession. \par \par \uc2  The yen was down around half a percent against the dollar and the euro, while the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained steady against the greenback from levels seen late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international bureau, Zembei Mizoguchi, said Japan was ready to take steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary, adding that rapid moves were not desirable. Meanwhile, a key gauge of Japanese corporate capital spending plummeted in January. \par \par \uc2  "We've had some absolutely horrid data. Private sector core machinery orders came out extremely weak showing. It's premature to expect recovery," said Jesper Dannesboe, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "On the back of that data it is understandable the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not to see the yen appreciate any more. You would see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it did." \par \par \uc2  At 0900 GMT, the dollar was trading at 129.17 yen, up around half a percent on the day, nearly three yen above last week's lows but still more than five yen below levels seen at the end of February. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also down around half a percent against the euro, trading around 112.84, more than a yen down from last week's highs which were fuelled by strong gains in Tokyo stocks. \par \par \uc2  The euro was rangebound against the dollar, trading around $0.8745. \par \par \uc2  TRADE SPAT \par \par \uc2  Analysts were also keeping a close eye on the ongoing dispute between the U.S. and many of its trading partners after Washington's decision to impose tariffs on steel imports. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Commerce Undersecretary Grant Aldonas told the Financial Times in an interview that other sectors could also face tariffs unless Europe and Japan reflate their economies. \par \par \uc2  "People will start to wonder how much they remain behind a strong dollar," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  MARKETS WARY OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Yen bulls were disappointed that Tokyo's Nikkei stock average could not sustain an early push above 12,000. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei finished the day down 33.51 points or 0.28 percent at 11,919.30. The sharp rise in Tokyo stocks in the last couple of weeks was one of the principal drivers behind the yen's sharp rally last week. \par \par \uc2  Stock market data showed overseas investors bought shares last week to the tune of a net 113.87 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials showed their displeasure last week over the yen's speedy rise, which eats into Japan's export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the MOF's Mizoguchi said, "We always have readiness to deal with various situations." \par \par \uc2  Dealers interpreted Mizoguchi's comments to mean the MOF was not content with the dollar's bounce from last week's near-three month low of 126.36 yen and wanted to see it higher. \par \par \uc2  Japan's core private-sector machinery orders, a key gauge of corporate capital spending, fell a worse-than-expected 15.6 percent in January from the previous month. \par \par \uc2  The market has been largely inured to gloomy Japanese data, but analysts noted the figure should be a leading indicator of investment and as such the data could suggest the economy will get worse before it gets better. \par \par \uc2  "You just get used to it, whether it's lousy or dire or whatever comes down in the rung after that," Hawkins said. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also getting no favours fron the whiff of a new corruption scandal hitting Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party which could further undermine public support for Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi and hamper his bid for structural reform. \par \par \uc2  A former top aide of one of the LDP's key reformers was arrested on Friday for allegedly evading income taxes. \par \par \uc2  ASSET ALLOCATION \par \par \uc2  Traders also waited for news from Japanese institutional investors, who are mapping out investment plans for the new business year. \par \par \uc2  A council under the Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare is set to announce its asset allocation plan late on Tuesday for the year starting on April 1. \par \par \uc2  Fresh funds to be allocated for next fiscal year are seen at around nine trillion yen and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is keen to find out what portion might be shifted to foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  At the end of last March, the Fund held about 1.06 trillion yen or 4.08 percent of its assets in foreign bonds. Total assets amounted to 27.5 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  "It's hard to think the asset allocation plan will change dramatically, but with fresh funds increasing, we are watching the impact," a foreign bank dealer in Tokyo said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020311dy3b00izo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
