\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen turns tail as Japan warns on rapid  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  moves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 12:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 8 (Reuters) - The yen halted a stunning seven-day rally against the dollar on Friday after Japan signalled it was unhappy with the speed of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rise. \par \par \uc2  In a bid to restore calm after the yen's biggest one-day leap against the dollar since October 1998, three senior Japanese officials warned action could be taken against sharp or speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. \par \par \uc2  The comments knocked Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back more than one percent from the three-month highs against the dollar and the euro in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  "The market is listening to the rhetoric coming out of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be ruled out," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "Much of the recent move in the yen has been driven by the unwinding of short positions, and there are doubts over how sustainable this rally is." \par \par \uc2  The yen dipped to session lows around 128.25 to the dollar and 112.75 to the euro after Japan's Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka quoted an official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  as saying Japan would take action against rapid moves in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Recent moves look like they are due in part to some speculative trading," he quoted the official as saying. "If the moves are too rapid, appropriate action will be taken as needed." \par \par \uc2  His comments chimed with similar warnings from Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa and vice-minister Haruhiko Kuroda earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  GLOBAL RECOVERY HOPES \par \par \uc2  Dealers are expecting U.S. jobs data later in the day to show further signs of a U.S. recovery, but they noted that recent strong data and Thursday's upbeat testimony from Federal Reserve chief Alan Greenspan have provided little benefit for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Rather \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are focusing on the benefits of a U.S. recovery on other regions around the world, particularly export-oriented Japan. \par \par \uc2  The euro has also benefited from investors' recent desire to diversify from U.S. assets, rising to six-week high around $0.8840 on Thursday. But the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  struggled to hold on to these gains on Friday as the dollar staged a sharper come-back against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/dollar is really a sideshow at the moment," said Bobby Jasper, senior dealer at Fuji Bank in London. "Dollar/yen moves are driving the euro's moves against both the dollar and the yen." \par \par \uc2  Citibank's Steven Saywell noted that while the euro was struggling to break out of its recent range against the dollar, it had significant upside potential longer-term. \par \par \uc2  "A strong recovery has already been priced into the dollar and U.S. equities and the euro could benefit as investors look for more attractive returns elsewhere," he said. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE JAWBONING \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials' comments helped the dollar limp off lows but traders said the remarks lacked urgency as they chose to comment on the pace of the yen's rise and not its level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also suspected the government's top priority was to keep stock prices strong ahead of the March 31 fiscal year-end and guessed Japan had no choice but to shoulder the yen's export-crimping rise for the time being. \par \par \uc2  "The yen's seen one heck of a move, rising four big figures in a day, so it's not surprising to see Japanese officials chipping in," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But Japan's priority going into the year end is on stocks, and I doubt they would actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it could harm the current buy-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  mentality." \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's key Nikkei share average rose above 12,000 for the first time in seven months on Friday, as investors continued to raise their exposure to Japanese assets. \par \par \uc2  Fourth quarter gross domestic product data showing Japan in its longest recession since 1993 was largely shrugged off as traders looked ahead to a cyclical pick-up, led by a strong recovery in the United States, which would bolster Japanese exports. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020308dy3800mzv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
