\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Kuroda: Weak Yen Won't Be Major G7 Topic -Yomiuri \b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2002\par 01:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Group of Seven financial leaders likely won't dwell on the weak yen when they meet this week, but the Bank of Japan's efforts to fight deflation will likely be in the spotlight, a top Japanese official was quoted as saying Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, also told the Yomiuri Shimbun in an interview that while the authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb an excessive rise or fall in the yen's value, such action isn't needed now. \par \par \uc2  G7 finance ministers and central bankers meet Friday and Saturday in Ottawa. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda indicated a nuanced government stance toward the BOJ, which will hold a monetary policy meeting Thursday and Friday. He kept up the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight deflation but admitted the BOJ has little room to maneuver. \par \par \uc2  "Deflation must be stopped," Japan's top financial diplomat told the mass-circulation newspaper. \par \par \uc2  Reiterating his oft-stated view that "deflation is a monetary phenomenon" and that monetary policy thus has a major role to play, he said, "I think this area will be a focus for the G7." \par \par \uc2  In this view, Kuroda aligned himself with U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill, who said in Tokyo two weeks ago that deflation "is a monetary phenomenon and will respond to monetary policy." \par \par \uc2  But Kuroda also expressed understanding for the limits of BOJ policy. \par \par \uc2  "The scope for monetary policy is limited, but as for the government, all we can say is that we'd like the Bank of Japan to continue doing its best." \par \par \uc2  Japan has been in and out of recession for a decade and stuck in deflation for two to four years, depending on which measure is used. The BOJ has slammed short-term interest rates to zero and flooded the banking system with more cash than it can use in a "quantitative easing." \par \par \uc2  Still, with Tokyo stocks at an 18-year low, the yen near a 40-month low and long-term interest rates at a one-year high - the first sustained triple bear market in 12 years - the pressure is on the BOJ to do more. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ believes further easing would have little effect, sources close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  say, but financial markets increasingly expect the BOJ will be forced to take such action as increasing its purchases of Japanese government bonds. \par \par \uc2  On \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kuroda, the point man for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, played down the yen's weakness, a sensitive point for policymakers. \par \par \uc2  The weak yen "won't be a major topic" at the Ottawa meeting, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry has made clear it welcomes the yen's decline from what it says was excessive strength last year, but officials have recently been at pains to say they aren't pushing the yen lower in a beggar-thy-neighbor devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Japan sold record amounts of yen for dollars and euros in September to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tokyo helped lift the dollar from a low of Y115.80 but stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around Y120. The dollar climbed as high as Y135.19 last Thursday. Around 0110 GMT Wednesday, it was trading for Y133.70. \par \par \uc2  "The yen, the dollar and the euro are traded in such huge volumes that it's impossible to control them as one might wish," Kuroda told the newspaper. \par \par \uc2  "We will take appropriate action in the event of excessive depreciation or appreciation of the yen, and for this reason we are closely watching the market," he said. \par \par \uc2  "But we don't plan to intentionally guide the yen lower to benefit our own economy," he said. "We aren't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we aren't in a situation where we need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Among \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Asian neighbors, China and South Korea have expressed concern about the yen's weakness. O'Neill warned Japan during his visit not to "manipulate" its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to weaken it and export the economy out of recession. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -Tokyo Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020206dy260000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
