\par\par \b \uc2  Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Feb. 1 \b0\par\par\uc2 2,069 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2002\par 10:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-415-4020. \par \par \uc2  MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japanese Finance Ministry officials expressed concern about the dollar's overnight surge, but their basic message remains the same: let the yen decline gradually. Finance Minister Shiokawa and key deputy Mizoguchi call the dollar's overnight surge to a 39-month high above Y135 sudden. But Vice Finance Minister Kuroda keeps the door open, saying the "adjustment" from previous dollar strength continues and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the yen at these levels. ("WRAP: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Signals Wants Yen To Keep Falling - Slowly," ran at 0640 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japan PM Koizumi's high-profile choice for foreign minister, former UNHCR Sadako Ogata, refuses the post, dealing another blow to Koizumi, whose popularity is crumbling after his unpopular sacking of Makiko Tanaka as Japan's top diplomat. ("WRAP: Ogata Snubs Japan PM Koizumi On Foreign Min Post," ran at 0224 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Bad loans held by Japanese banks rose in the six months ended Sept. 30, Japan's Financial Services Agency said, underscoring the country's lack of progress in restoring its financial system to health. ("Japan FSA/NPLs," first take ran at 0830 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: S&P hasn't altered its view on Japan's sovereign credit and sees no immediate need for any changes to its rating on Japan, the agency's Asia sovereign ratings director says in the wake of a grim S&P report on Japan's credit. ("S&P: View On Japan Hasn't Changed Since Last Rating Cut," ran at 0600 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =BOJ WATCH: The Bank of Japan insists further monetary easing couldn't yank the economy out of deflation and recession, but a recent jump in long-term interest rates may force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to think again. The threat of a financial crisis and burgeoning government debt have spooked long-placid bond investors into dumping Japanese government bonds, sending the 10-year yield to a nine-month high near 1.5%. ("=BOJ WATCH: Long-Term Rate Rise A Wake-Up Call For BOJ," ran at 0712 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE: The housing construction boom that has buoyed Australia's economy amidst the global downturn looks set for a slowdown, with residential building approvals having fallen sharply in December. But despite coming in well below expectations, data not expected to rekindle hopes for further monetary easing as the housing sector has long been forecast to slow in mid-2002. ("WRAP/Australia Building," first take ran at 0120 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI: China Huarong Asset Management Corp. has recently submitted an application to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to issue asset-backed securities in China, in the latest development in the race by financial firms to float China's first ABS. ("Huarong AMC Applies To Issue ABSs; Could Be 1st In China," ran at 0416 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korea's exports continued to contract in January, but the pace of decline moderated to 8.9% on year after seven months of double-digit declines. However, the government cautioned against premature optimism, noting that a full-fledged recovery in exports is likely to take place only in the second half. ("WRAP: S Korea Jan Export Fall Slows, Govt Still Cautious," ran at 0822 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: The push for financial services sector consolidation in Asia is expected to drive merger and acquisition activity in the region this year, although cross-border interest from the U.S. remains uncertain as corporate America deals with the recession and a string of accounting scandals. (Story expected by 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: Thailand's consumer price index rose marginally in January from the previous month, following three consecutive monthly declines, but compared with a year earlier inflation remained subdued at below 1%. ("DATA SNAP:Thai Jan CPI Rises Marginally Amid Low Prices," ran at 0834 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: Japan's yen will have to undergo a significant and sustained slide before China seriously considers a devaluation of the yuan, Lehman Brothers senior political analyst Alastair Newton tells Dow Jones. ("INTERVIEW: Lehman Analysts Point To Stronger China Yuan," ran at 0711 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Life has returned to normal in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets: everyone agrees that the yen has room to weaken further. The dollar surged late Thursday in European and U.S. trade as remarks from U.S. Treasury Secretary O'Neill and Japan's top financial diplomat prompted yen sales. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Japan MOF Still Targets Gradual Yen Weakness," ran at 0221 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar poked above the Y135 level again early in the Asian session Friday, inviting profit-taking that took it back below late Thursday's New York levels. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," first take ran at 0534 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  wrap expected after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Philippine state-owned National Power, or Napocor, has decided to pay a high price to diversify its investor base. (Story expected by 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: Hopes for a deal between Hynix Semiconductor and Micron Technology were revived after a statement from Hynix's main creditor, Korea Exchange Bank. "Hynix is trying its best to close a deal with Micron to sell its chip production facilities," said KEB. Micron is scheduled to hold an analysts call Friday morning U.S. time, in which the U.S. chipmaker is expected to disclose details on the state of its talks with Hynix. ("WRAP: S Korea Jan Export Fall Slows, Govt Still Cautious," ran at 0758 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japan's FSA orders Morgan Stanley Japan to suspend stock trading on its own account for 24 business days for short-selling violations. MOF suspends the firm from JGB underwriting and auctions. ("FSA/Morgan Stanley Japan," first take ran at 0727 GMT; "MOF/Morgan Stanley Japan," expected shortly) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: NEC shares sink 11% in heavy trading as the electronics giant reported a Y155 billion quarterly group net loss and an analyst downgraded the stock. (Story expected shortly) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE: Australia-based resources conglomerate BHP Billiton says it has successfully teamed with Anglo American and Glencore International to acquire ExxonMobil's Colombian thermal coal \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Intercor. The \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  owns a 50% stake in the Cerrejon Zona Norte coal mining \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as rail and port infrastructure in the region. BHP Billiton says Intercor has a net asset value of US$366 million. But analysts speculate that Exxon's stake could command around US$600 million. ("WRAP: BHP Billiton, Anglo, Glencore Buy Colombia Coal Op," ran at 0702 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: Taiwan's United Microelectronics won't see an earnings boost or a significant rise in orders this year from its latest tie-up with U.S. chip giant Advanced Micro Devices, but the advanced technology should help it close the gap with key rival Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing. Advanced Micro Devices and United Microelectronics will establish a joint venture to build an advanced chip-manufacturing plant in Singapore. (Story expected after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing, the world's largest chip foundry, and Macronix International, a Taiwanese maker of flash memory chips, priced offerings at levels that point to investor optimism over the technology sector. TSMC's ADR issue was priced at a wafer-thin 1.3% discount to its ADR's closing price in New York Thursday. Macronix announced a premium of 20% for its US$140-million convertible bond, one of the highest conversion premiums for a Taiwan technology company. ("WRAP:Taiwan TSMC, Macronix Pricing Point To Tech Optimism," ran at 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Creative Technology's share price has doubled in the space of a month on strong second-quarter earnings and hopes riding on its new soundscard product, but the dizzying rise may soon fizzle out, analysts and fund managers say. ("Singapore: Creative's Rally May Soon Fizzle Out - Analysts," ran at 0518 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Creative Technology's shares tumbled 15.8% to S$23.50 as perceptions grew that the soundcard maker's stock had gotten too far ahead of itself, traders say. (WRAP will run shortly) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japanese sales of new cars, trucks and buses dropped for the fifth straight month in January as consumer sentiment weakens and unemployment hits record highs amid steadily worsening economic conditions. Sales as measured by registrations fell 2.6% in January from the same month last year to 248,489 units. ("Japan January Auto Sales," first take ran at 0521 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korean auto makers report higher January sales figures. Hyundai's jumped 39% on year; Daewoo's rose 2.9%; Kia's were up 7.2%; Ssangyong's soared 58%. (WRAP expected after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI: Aggressive price cuts by China's car makers - as much as 20% for some models since the start of the year - haven't translated into a meaningful rise in sales and that is worrying analysts. ("China Car Makers Slash Prices, But Response Lukewarm," ran at 0721 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: Chinese-language Internet portal Sohu.com has responded to rival Sina.com's allegations of plagiarism by filing a countersuit against Sina, intensifying the highly public spat between the two Nasdaq-listed companies. ("WRAP: Sohu Countersues Sina, Settles Homeway Dispute," ran at 0427 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: Taiwan chip maker Winbond Electronics says its losses widened in the fourth quarter from the third, dragging it into the red for 2001 amid a severe industry downturn. But with signs of a recovery in the DRAM market, Winbond expects revenue to be better in the first quarter. ("Taiwan/Winbond/Earnings," first take ran at 0729 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: Delta Electronics, Thailand's leading computer parts producer, says the damage from the collapse of one of its plants is limited because the company's premises and equipment are insured. ("EXCLUSIVE: Thai Delta Elec," first take ran at 0525 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MANILA: Philippine food and beverage group San Miguel is expected to report lower net profit for 2001 due largely to lower interest income as money went into acquisitions. In 2000 San Miguel posted a net profit of 7.5 billion pesos, but when non-recurring income of PHP670 million was excluded, its net profit dwindled to PHP6.84 billion. Many analysts say the company will be hard pressed to match even that figure in 2001. ("Philippines' San Miguel Likely To Post Lower '01 Net Pft," ran at 0657 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIAN IPO FOCUS: Bharti Tele-Ventures' initial public offering may well become a benchmark for the valuation of other private telecom companies in India and could breathe new life into the moribund IPO market. Most expect the IPO to be oversubscribed and debut above its INR45 floor price. But the stock may trade lower in the months ahead. ("=ASIAN IPO FOCUS:Bharti Tele-Ventures Could Set Benchmark," ran at 0402 GMT) \par \par \uc2  ENERGY/COMMODITIES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Kuwait's 860,000-barrel-a-day spare crude oil production capacity should cushion any supply shortages that may arise after an explosion at a northern oil gathering facility in the oil-rich state Thursday, traders and industry officials say. News of the blast at state-owned Kuwait Oil's crude oil gathering center in al-Rawdhatain boosted after-hours Access crude futures prices on the New York Mercantile Exchange and Asian oil product prices. ("WRAP:Fears Recede That Kuwait Blast May Disrupt Oil Flow," ran at 0550 GMT; a correction ran at 0709 GMT) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON: As the deadline looms for U.S.-based Edison Mission Energy's NZ$1.2 billion takeover offer for New Zealand's Contact Energy Ltd., the prognosis has hardly changed, and it isn't something Edison would like to hear ("Edison Mission Losing Grip On NZ Contact Takeover - Mkt," ran at 0002 GMT) \par \par \uc2  With only two days left until the close of its offer, acceptances for Edison Mission Energy's NZ$1.2 billion takeover bid for New Zealand's Contact Energy are still far short of target. ("Edison Still Far Short Of Target For NZ Contact Takeover," ran at 0240 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: State-run Korea Electric Power Corp. says it is reevaluating the stake sale processes for its units, Korea Power Engineering, or Kopec, and Korea Plant Service & Engineering, or KPS, after two auctions have failed. ("S Korea's Kepco Reviews Stake Sale Plans For Units," ran at 0642 GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020201dy21000kh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
