\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen gains, Swiss franc sought after India comments. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2002\par 12:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 11 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped around a third of a percent against the yen on Friday after the pace of its recent rally unnerved both Japanese authorities and dealers already cautious ahead of a speech by Fed chief Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar continued to trade sideways against the euro, dealers said it was losing ground to safe haven buying in the Swiss franc after India's army chief said his army was fully prepared for any conventional war with Pakistan but was ready to respond in kind should Islamabad use nuclear weapons. \par \par \uc2  While markets were closely watching developments in the region the fate of the yen remained the biggest talking point. In recent days, Japanese officials have changed their tune from apparent nonchalance over the yen's sharp fall - it has dropped 10 percent in two months - to seeming concern. \par \par \uc2  "Really there has been a change in tone from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . People were a little bit surprised about it and overnight we had another confirmation from the trade minister," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, economist at CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading at 132.10 to the dollar and 117.87 to the euro at 1115 GMT. The euro was little changed from the New York close against the dollar at around $0.8918. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was hovering around intraday highs against the dollar at 1.6582 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN TRADE MINSTER ON YEN \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Japanese authorities were probably trying to make sure the yen did not go into freefall rather than actively trying to make their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise. \par \par \uc2  "Many people from different viewpoints are wondering now whether a rapid fall in the yen is appropriate," Japanese Trade Minister Takeo Hirunama told reporters. "(The dollar) is nearing 135 yen and I think this is the limit." \par \par \uc2  Hirunama's remarks came on the heels of comments from Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa who said on Thursday that the pace of the yen's decline might have been too rapid. \par \par \uc2  Finance Ministry comment on the yen is taken very seriously by investors as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught the market which was short yen," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Japanese economics minister Heizo Takenaka is scheduled to speak later in the day in London. \par \par \uc2  Dealers speculated the government was growing nervous the yen's fall could trigger a slump in Japanese stock and bond prices - and even a full-blown economic crisis somewhere down the road. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Asian neighbours have also expressed concern over the weak yen because it makes companies less competitive with Japanese firms. China and South Korea have been among the loudest objectors. \par \par \uc2  Traders were being doubly cautious ahead of an evening speech by Fed chairman Greenspan, who is one of a number of Fed officials talking during the day. \par \par \uc2  "The expectations are that he will be moderately dovish. But it's a difficult line to toe - talking up the economy while not upsetting the bond markets too much," Hawkins said. \par \par \uc2  INDIA PAKISTAN DISPUTE IN VIEW \par \par \uc2  The latest salvo in the war of words between India and Pakistan was fired by India's army chief General S. Padmanabhan. \par \par \uc2  With hundreds of thousands of troops massed along the India-Pakistan border, India's army chief said on Friday his army was ready for a conventional war with Pakistan - or a nuclear one if need be. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the Swiss franc was the obvious candidate for any safe haven flows. \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't be surprised if the Swiss franc benefits in any worrying conflict situation," said Childe-Freeman. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020111dy1b00m2j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
