\par\par \b \uc2  Yen resumes descent, Takenaka gives nod to bears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2002\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The yen scurried to a fresh three-year low against the dollar on Tuesday after Japanese officials again appeared to give the market a green light to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Commenting on the yen's fall, Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka told economists in Washington, "It's a decision made by the markets and I don't think that decision is that far off economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  He also said U.S. leaders seemed to expect a relatively rapid recovery in their country's economy and contrasted that with the downside risks in recession-bound Japan. \par \par \uc2  Dealers are paying close attention to comments from Takenaka during his visit to the United States, hoping for hints on the U.S. attitude to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  "His comments gave the market the impression that Takenaka said to the U.S. that Japan wants a weaker yen and that the U.S. okayed that," said Koji Fukaya, chief analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, Deputy Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda added fuel to the fire when he told Jiji news agency that he still saw the yen's weakness as a correction to reflect economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Their comments surprised many dealers who had expected Japanese officials to tone down their rhetoric ahead of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's visit to Southeast Asia, which begins on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Asian nations are growingly nervous about the yen's recent slump, as it would help Japanese exporters at the expense of Asian rivals. Already, South Korea and China have voiced concerns about the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  "Many dealers were expecting some sort of adjustment of the dollar," said Takashi Toyahara, manager at Nomura Securities. \par \par \uc2  "So they are all being forced to cover short positions," he added. \par \par \uc2  The greenback shot up to 132.75 yen by afternoon trade, well up on late New York levels around 130.97. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also helped by the Nikkei average, which lost 2.26 percent to 10,695.60, adding to the gloomy sentiment on Japan's economy and rekindling talk that foreign capital may be deserting Japanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  HAYAMI DISSENTS \par \par \uc2  The euro also firmed against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by more than one yen to 118.37 yen compared with levels around 116.97 in late New York, but still some distance away from its 28-month peak hit last week at 119.72. \par \par \uc2  The euro dipped slightly against the dollar to trade around $0.8919/24 in late morning, compared with $0.8931 at the New York close. \par \par \uc2  While many Japanese officials made no secret of their satisfaction with the yen's fall, Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami is fighting a lone battle against it. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, currently in Switzerland at a meeting of the Bank for International Settlements, was reported by Japan's Jiji news agency as saying the BOJ could not accept the yen's recent depreciation. \par \par \uc2  But analysts pointed out that Hayami is not the main voice of authority on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "First of all, it is not the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...and secondly Hayami's opinion is not necessarily shared by everyone else at the BOJ," said Marshall Gittler, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of America. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0658 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                              07 Jan.\par                     -----------------------------------------\par   Euro/dlr              0.8922   0.8933   -0.12    -6.04   0.9496\par   Dlr/yen               132.53   130.96   +1.20   +15.80   114.45\par   Euro/yen              118.21   116.99   +1.04    +8.74   108.71\par   Dlr/swiss             1.6502   1.6487   +0.09    +3.06   1.6012\par   Stg/dlr               1.4390   1.4393   -0.02    -4.07   1.5000\par   Can/dlr               1.6007   1.5988   +0.12    +7.33   1.4914\par   Aus/dlr               0.5193   0.5175   +0.35    -7.17   0.5594\par   Euro/swiss            1.4725   1.4729   -0.03    -3.26   1.5221\par   Euro/stg              0.6196   0.6205   -0.15    -2.04   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 0658 GMT. All data taken from Reuters\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 07 Jan.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.8922   0.8933   -0.12    -6.04   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               132.53   130.96   +1.20   +15.80   114.45\par  Euro/yen              118.21   116.99   +1.04    +8.74   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss             1.6502   1.6487   +0.09    +3.06   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr               1.4390   1.4393   -0.02    -4.07   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.6007   1.5988   +0.12    +7.33   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.5193   0.5175   +0.35    -7.17   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4725   1.4729   -0.03    -3.26   1.5