\par\par \b \uc2  Yen stumbles after Takenaka  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  comments. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Isabel Reynolds \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2002\par 03:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against the yen on Tuesday morning, helped by comments from Japan's economics minister that its movements were not far off fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Commenting on the yen's recent fall to a three-year low at 132.30 to the dollar, Heizo Takenaka told economists in Washington, "It's a decision made by the markets and I don't think that decision is that far off economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  He also said U.S. leaders seemed to expect a relatively rapid recovery in their country's economy and pointed out the downside risks in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Dealers are paying close attention to comments from Takenaka during his visit to the United States, hoping for hints on the U.S. attitude to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  Thanks to Takenaka's verbal support, the greenback was trading at around 131.82 yen by late morning, well up on late New York levels around 130.97 and within sight of last week's three-year high. \par \par \uc2  "If it manages to break right through that high, the dollar will return to its bullish trend. But if it holds at current levels, we are still in the position-adjustment phase," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  He pointed out that many in the market were expecting repatriation of Japanese funds ahead of the financial year-end in March, and such flows would likely support the yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro also firmed against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the morning to stand at around 117.45 yen compared with levels around 116.97 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  HAYAMI DISSENTS \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that though there was nothing new in Takenaka's comments, they came in contrast to earlier remarks by Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, currently in Switzerland at a meeting of the Bank for International Settlements, was reported by Japan's Jiji news agency as saying the BOJ could not accept the yen's recent depreciation. \par \par \uc2  "Although that's something he always says, it does emphasise the fact that the yen's fall has been very rapid," said the Japanese bank dealer. The yen has skidded around 12 cents against the dollar since early November. \par \par \uc2  But many analysts were keen to point out that Hayami's was not the voice of authority on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "First of all, it is not the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...and secondly Hayami's opinion is not necessarily shared by everyone else at the BOJ," said Marshall Gittler, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Ken Landon of Deutsche Bank agreed. "When there is a conflict of statements about the yen" it pays to focus on what top Finance Ministry officials say, he said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  The euro dipped slightly against the dollar to trade around $0.8914 in late morning, compared with $0.8931 at the New York close. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0212 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                              07 Jan.\par                     -----------------------------------------\par   Euro/dlr              0.8912   0.8933   -0.24    -6.15   0.9496\par   Dlr/yen               131.8