\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Mutoh:  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Should Reflect Fundamentals \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2001\par 03:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Nikkei/Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not thinking of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at this time, though generally speaking it is necessary to take steps against sudden moves in the market, the top bureaucrat at the ministry said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It is necessary to respond to sudden moves in the market, but we are not thinking of anything in particular at this time," Toshiro Mutoh, administrative vice finance minister, said. \par \par \uc2  His comments indicate the ministry is not ready to step in to stem the dollar's sharp rise just yet, even in the face of concerns stock and bond prices may suffer from a rapid yen fall. \par \par \uc2  Mutoh's remarks came after the dollar continued to rise against the yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday, extending sharp gains from recent weeks. The dollar has risen over 5% against the yen since the beginning of December. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think our position on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  has changed," Mutoh told reporters before a meeting at the prime minister's official residence, indicating the MOF maintains its stance that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Mutoh declined to comment on specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -Tokyo Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011227d