\par\par \b \uc2  Keidanren/Yen -2: After Dollar Rises Above Y131 \b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2001\par 07:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Nikkei/Dow Jones)--A sharp fall in the yen is undesirable for Japanese companies, the head of Japan's leading business lobby said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "A rapid fall in the yen is not desirable for companies," said Takashi Imai, the chairman of Keidanren, or the Japan Federation of Economic Organization. \par \par \uc2  Imai, who is also chairman of Nippon Steel Corp. (J.NIP or 5401), spoke after the dollar rose above Y131 earlier Wednesday for the first time in over three years. \par \par \uc2  Altogether, the yen has lost more than 5% against the dollar since early December amid deteriorating economic conditions, falling share prices, and downgrades of Japan's sovereign credit rating by international credit agencies. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market perceptions that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is happy to see the yen decline to help support the economy have also contributed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening. \par \par \uc2  "A rapid yen fall is not desirable. It means Japan is being sold, and it will also lead to lower credibility for Japanese government bonds," Imai sad. \par \par \uc2  But he indicated that the current levels of the dollar against the yen should satisfy both the U.S. and Japanese governments. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar at around Y130 is a level both the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can accept without having to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the markets)," he added. \par \par \uc2  "Regardless of whether it is stronger or weaker, it is not desirable for corporate earnings for market moves to be rapid," Imai added, expressing concern that the recent sharp fall in the yen might affect earnings. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen is often thought to benefit Japanese exporters as it increases the repatriated yen value of overseas sales. A stronger yen, on the other hand, generally benefits importers by reducing the yen cost of their goods. \par \par \uc2  As for the possibility of Japan facing the triple weakness in the markets of a falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declining share prices, and lower bond prices, Imai said "that danger is always there." \par \par \uc2  "(The economy) will come close to a hard landing, but it will be important for bad loan disposals to be carried out without forcing many companies to fail," Imai said, emphasizing that structural reforms should continue even though they may not be easy. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -Tokyo Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011226dxcq0008n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
