\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-BOJ maverick slams fellow board members. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2001\par 12:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 11 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan's maverick board member rapped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's conservative decision-making on Wednesday, saying radical action is needed to halt deflation and hoist the economy out of recession. \par \par \uc2  Nobuyuki Nakahara, whose aggressive easing proposals have been voted down at countless Policy Board meetings over the past few years, said the economy was in such dire straits that the BOJ must take unprecedented measures, such as buying foreign bonds or using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps. \par \par \uc2  Such measures effectively increase the amount of yen in circulation but have been controversial due to the impact on the yen and concerns over turf battle with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He also said that the economy is on the verge of a deflationary spiral. \par \par \uc2  With interest rates already near zero, the BOJ must seek to change the market's expectations on price trends and even use a weaker yen that might result from foreign bond transactions to revive the economy, Nakahara said in the speech. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is in an extremely tough situation and is likely to have entered the first stage of a deflationary spiral. The current monetary policy stance is only passively reacting to liquidity demand and is insufficient," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Therefore, I think we need to expand our quantitative easing policy through feasible and less problematic measures, and one major option is to buy foreign bonds." \par \par \uc2  In a 100-minute-long speech, Nakahara berated the nine-member board's past policy decisions, saying their easing steps had been persistently "too little, too late." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's so-called "quantitative easing" policy, adopted in March, had been proposed by Nakahara a long time ago but shunned as impossible, and his opposition to the BOJ's decision to raise interest rates in August 2000 has proved correct, he added. \par \par \uc2  By playing down unconventional policy steps, even after adopting them, many of his fellow BOJ board members were doing little to enhance the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility, he argued. \par \par \uc2  "Such a behaviour could give the public the impression that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in self-denial," Nakahara said. \par \par \uc2  The need for a radical easing was urgent now, Nakahara said, noting that last week's gross domestic product (GDP) data had confirmed Japan had slid into recession. \par \par \uc2  A drop in exports has depressed output and capital spending, and in turn pushed up unemployment to a record 5.4 percent and made consumers nervous about spending. \par \par \uc2  FOREIGN BOND CONTROVERSY \par \par \uc2  Nakahara, the former chairman of oil refiner Tonen Corp who turned 67 on Tuesday, repeated his challenge to the BOJ board to be bolder. \par \par \uc2  Nakahara said the BOJ should raise its quantitative easing target, expressed as the balance of banks' current account deposits at the BOJ, to 10 trillion yen ($80 billion) from "above six trillion yen" and boost it to 12-15 trillion if needed. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has already been supplying such levels of funds in reality, but to do so within the "above six trillion" framework amounted to merely keeping money market interest rates steady and did not have the desired "announcement effect", he said. \par \par \uc2  He said a change in the overnight rate to 0.004 percent from 0.002 percent only meant 550 yen difference for a 10 billion yen transaction and had negligible impact on the macro economy. \par \par \uc2  As one way of boosting liquidity supply to the yen money market, the BOJ should buy foreign bonds, he added. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ were to print more yen and exchange it for dollars to buy U.S. Treasuries in the market, for instance, the transaction would be a transfer of yen funds to the private sector from the public sector and have a monetary easing effect. \par \par \uc2  But at the same time, that would likely have a direct impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - something that does not go down well with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has the jurisdiction over Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Nakahara said monthly foreign bond purchases of 200 billion yen would be a drop in the ocean for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and would not be regarded as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should take advantage of any yen weakness resulting from monetary easing, given its economic recovery was likely to be export-driven, judging from bleak domestic factors. \par \par \uc2  However, Nakahara said he was opposed to even more radical ideas that have come up such as buying stocks or real estate. \par \par \uc2  He said such assets were not standardised and liquid instruments suited for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ($1=125.96 Yen) (Additional reporting by Tamawa Kadoya). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011211dxcb00mp7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
