\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Huge  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves just another problem for Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 1,168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2001\par 07:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 11 (Reuters) - For most countries, it would be a measure of economic virility; but for Japan, holding the world's biggest pile of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is becoming a major economic headache with risks that are growing by the month. \par \par \uc2  Far from good news, the steadily rising reserves not only serve little purpose but are actually adding to an already intolerably high debt load, economists and fund managers say. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $403.880 billion at end-November - four times the level of seven years ago. By comparison, U.S. reserves stand at about $69.155 billion. \par \par \uc2  Because they are held predominantly in dollars, the reserves are vulnerable to a fall in the value of the dollar or of any narrowing in the U.S.-Japan interest rate differential. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at this level do not appear to be sustainable. Japan has to think about ways to bring it down," said Sayuri Kawamura, senior economist at the Japan Research Institute (JRI) economics department. \par \par \uc2  If Japan does not find a way to reduce its official U.S. dollar exposure, only a small fall in the dollar's value against the yen would translate into massive losses. \par \par \uc2  "At least we should diversify the risk of holding the reserves almost all in the dollar," said Yoshiichi Taguchi, manager of global investment at Tokio Marine and Fire Insurance. \par \par \uc2  The problem is, the only way to reduce the dollar reserves, or to reposition the reserves away from the dollar, would be to sell off huge amounts of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - a move that policymakers fear could strain relations with Washington. \par \par \uc2  The rapid growth in reserves has been left unchecked until now by the government's support of the dollar, which it justifies as support for Japan's export-oriented industrial structure. \par \par \uc2  In theory, a weak dollar undermines the export competitiveness of Japanese products. However, that's not the only explanation for the ever-rising volume of dollar reserves. \par \par \uc2  The mammoth reserves reflect Japan's role in supporting the dollar as it steadily declined after the collapse of the fixed-rate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in 1971. In April 1995 the dollar hit a record low of 79.75 yen, versus 126 yen today. \par \par \uc2  OFF-BUDGET FUNDING \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been considered a virtue as it brings the state a handsome revenue," said Kawamura of JRI. \par \par \uc2  Japan uses dollars bought from the market to buy U.S. Treasury securities, which bring in hefty income gains. \par \par \uc2  The build-up of reserves does not come cheap for a country that already has the industrialised world's biggest public debt of around 130 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  To fund dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues special short-term paper called financing bills (FBs) to procure yen funds to give to the banks in exchange for the dollars bought. \par \par \uc2  The net income from coupon gains on U.S. Treasury holdings less interest payments on FBs was nearly two trillion yen ($15.93 billion) in the financial year that ended on March 31. \par \par \uc2  That income goes into a special \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account that will provide 1.37 trillion yen to the state budget this year, the biggest contribution from 37 such off-budget accounts, which also include, for example, funds from the state postal savings system. \par \par \uc2  The outstanding volume of FBs was 44.27 trillion yen in June and is estimated to have reached nearly 50 trillion yen ($397 billion) after Japan's aggressive dollar buying in September. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has pledged to keep new debt issuance to 30 trillion yen for the current fiscal year and the next, but the special accounts are not covered by this. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  MANIPULATION \par \par \uc2  Nobody in official circles has stood up to criticise the dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which brings the government such good revenue, even though it has sometimes been overdone and is poorly disciplined, Kawamura said. \par \par \uc2  In September, Japan spent a hefty $26 billion to cap the yen's rise against the dollar and euro. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are sceptical that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has any big effect in the longer term and say it distorts natural supply and demand in the short term while stimulating speculative activity. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts believe the authorities, despite their statements to the contrary, are trying to manipulate rates, not merely smooth market movements. \par \par \uc2  "The massive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are clear evidence that Japan has aggressively tried to manipulate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than smooth a dollar fall," said a fund manager in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Now that the reserves have become so large due to the lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  discipline, the only way to reduce the dollar reserves is to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  That has not been a desirable option recently, when the authorities have been trying to hold down the yen, but even when dollar sales have been possible, Japan always seems to have held back out of deference to the United States, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's reserves kept rising because Tokyo has long been unable to do reserve management on its own for fear of offending the United States," Taguchi of Tokio Marine said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last conducted dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June 1998 when the dollar was over 142 yen, compared with 126 today. \par \par \uc2  But it took months before the U.S. authorities gave the go-ahead, monetary sources said. Furthermore, the amount of dollar-selling is historically small compared with dollar-buying and Japan only sold $1.614 billion at that time. \par \par \uc2  HIDDEN LOSSES \par \par \uc2  There are other problems with the current policy. \par \par \uc2  Kawamura said the cherished revenue from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account has been generated thanks to the wide interest rate gap between the United States and Japan. \par \par \uc2  The yield differential between 10-year U.S. Treasuries and Japanese government bonds was at 375 basis points on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "In the future, it is possible for the gap to totally disappear or for Japanese rates to go higher than those of the U.S.," Kawamura said. \par \par \uc2  If that happened, the government would lose what has become a vital source of funding. \par \par \uc2  The gap has been sustained by Japan's ultra-loose monetary policy, but the U.S. Federal Reserve has also slashed key short-term rates this year and will likely cut further if the economy continues to stagnate. \par \par \uc2  The evaporation of the rate gap would put the spotlight on the peculiar accounting system used for the special \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  account, unlike anything used in the private sector. \par \par \uc2  On the flow side the special \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts generate handsome profits, but on the stock side they incur huge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses that can accumulate over decades. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the account has nearly 10.5 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, accumulated as it has bought the dollar through its fall from above 200 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The state needs appropriate asset and liability management, just like the private sector," Kawamura said. ($1=125.53 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011211dxcb00d9t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
