\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Miki:Foreign Bond Buying One Possible Future Option \b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2001\par 04:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAPPORO, Japan -(Dow Jones)- Foreign-bond purchases and yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are two possible future options the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government could consider to boost the sagging economy and liquidity levels, said BOJ policy board member Toshio Miki Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Once deflation has gone and monetary conditions are normalized he BOJ should also consider setting an outright price target, Miki said. \par \par \uc2  "The impact of the quantitative easing policy hasn't fully permeated through the economy...there is a limit to what monetary policy can achieve alone," Miki said in a speech to business leaders in Sapporo, northern Japan. \par \par \uc2  The government and the BOJ "could consider a monetary and fiscal policy mix which includes spending, JGB (Japanese government bond) purchases and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy - including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and foreign-bond buying - all of which lie close to the realms of fiscal policy," the moderately dovish BOJ policy-maker said. \par \par \uc2  Last March, the BOJ switched to a policy of targeting the quantity of money in the financial system and currently guides commercial bank reserves "above Y6 trillion" - in practice keeping the level around Y9 trillion. This has sent short-term interest rates to virtually zero, but despite flooding the banks with liquidity, deflation is getting worse. \par \par \uc2  Miki said it is worth consider buying commercial paper, corporate bonds and asset-backed securities in the framework of keeping market mechanisms functioning. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering buying commercial paper outright.....as a way to improve liquidity provision to the money market," he said. \par \par \uc2  "We need to always check needs for funds and continue injecting ample liquidity." \par \par \uc2  Miki said purchases of real estate and stocks are future options but said they weren't on the agenda now. \par \par \uc2  Miki also said it is necessary to create a numerical price forecast or goal to achieve stability in prices. \par \par \uc2  However, he ruled out a quick implementation, saying that the BOJ's ability to provide monetary stimulus is very limited considering short-term interest rates are near zero. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, monetary policy has been blunted by structural problems in the economy, such as banks' mountain of bad loans. \par \par \uc2  Miki said appropriate government fiscal spending is necessary to help make up for the slump in private demand. \par \par \uc2  "The biggest subject that Japan faces is to get out of deflation. To achieve the goal, fiscal spending is necessary," Miki said. \par \par \uc2  Reiterating the BOJ's line, Miki said a sustainable recovery in Japan and stable prices won't be achieved until the banking system regains health and banks start lending normally again. \par \par \uc2  The bad-loan problem continues to impede the effectiveness of the BOJ's super-easy monetary policy, Miki said. \par \par \uc2  "Whether banks will be able to increase capital through securitization of loan assets and restructuring remains a vital issue," Miki said. "If major banks can't increase their capital by themselves a (further) injection of public funds will be necessary." \par \par \uc2  Banks that hold back from discarding their dud loans or those that let their capital levels fall will likely see the value of their share price fall and invite bankruptcy, Miki said. \par \par \uc2  Price falls may be accelerating, but Miki said the Japanese economy hasn't entered a deflationary spiral. It is widely thought that the threat of a deflationary downdraft would push the BOJ into taking more radical measures. \par \par \uc2  Miki sounded a gloomy tone on the outlook for the economy, noting that exports, output and conditions in the household sector continue to deteriorate. \par \par \uc2  The relative stability in personal spending compared to other parts of the economy is a key reason preventing the economy from falling into a deflationary spiral, Miki said \par \par \uc2  He added that a rebound in the struggling IT sector is needed to help pull the world's second-largest economy out of its slump. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ's policy board next meets Dec. 18-19, the last policy-decision\par meeting in 2001. Next week the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  releases its closely-eyed\par December tankan survey of business conditions.\par   -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-9203;\par hiroshi.inoue@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011206dxc60005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
