\par\par \b \uc2  FXAnalytics Focus:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  By Any Other Name \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Gilmore \par \uc2 969 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2001\par 20:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics \par \par \uc2  We are told by Koizumi cabinet ministers and senior LDP officials alike that Japan should engage in the purchasing of foreign bonds pronto to counter deflation. But calling it foreign bond buying is disingenuous and suggests authorities need to up their marketing budget. Call it what it is, for gosh sakes...\b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Most central banks, often under orders from finance ministries like \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and U.S. Treasury, invest foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  obtained in the open market in foreign bonds of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased. They yield more than overnight balances. Japan owns more foreign bonds than any other country thanks to MoF ordering record \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last two decades (at end-October foreign reserves exceeded $405bln). \par \par \uc2  Is this proposal more of the same? Yes and no. Yes, it is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has frequently turned to in recent years to prevent a yen overshoot (too strong) and the dollars and euros accumulated are largely invested in U.S. treasuries and European government bonds. No, in that government and LDP officials are arguing that the BoJ use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase the money supply permanently. In other words, conduct unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BoJ may have left some of its September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, but not enough to make a difference to money market conditions. Normally the BoJ, as do all G7 central banks, would mop up the yen it puts into the system via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with bill sales leaving money stock unchanged. But daily \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without commensurate bill sales would balloon the money stock, i.e., the amount of yen in the system. In effect the BoJ would be printing yen, a characteristic often associated with hyperinflation. However, the BoJ (and U.S. Treasury) argues it is already keeping the money market flush with liquidity while banks burdened with bad loans are not lending it and individuals and firms worried about the economic outlook are not eager to take on loans. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ remains opposed to the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposal. Minutes from the October 29 board meeting showed an 8-1 vote against the proposal, with only the maverick Nakahara voting in favor of it (he after all proposed it). No doubt Nakahara was the source behind last week's FT article that brought the issue into focus as it said the BoJ was considering the move and it had backing at senior levels in the Bush administration. Nakahara has also proposed inflation targeting and outright government bond purchases. These proposals are regularly defeated 8-1. Governor Hayami and the majority of the BoJ board members are adamantly opposed to the plan as it would represent a radical departure in monetary policy, and risk a step down the slippery slope of promoting inflation and ultimately risking hyperinflation. Sure, hyperinflation is not on the radar screen, but Hayami is not ready to buy into hyperinflation as a cure for deflation. No G7 central banker would. \par \par \uc2  Hayami and U.S. Treasury also question what a sharply weaker yen would do for exports when the global economy is in recession. Declines in the yen would have to be significant to see exports respond, compensating for weak foreign demand. And we have noted before that Japan's neighbors in the region would reject a weak yen policy by Japan as it would threaten Asian competitiveness generally and risk igniting competitive devaluations. \par \par \uc2  Then there is the question of how much \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be needed to drive the yen significantly lower? Nakahara proposed JY200bln a month at the October 29 board meeting. This is about $1.6bln a month or $80mln a day. This is a drop in the bucket. Over seven days in September the BoJ sold JPY3.146trln for dollars ($26bln at 117/$) and JPY65.2 bln for euros (eur600mln at 107/euro). In other words, what Nakahara has in mind is a drop in the bucket. Surely government and LDP officials have far larger number in mind...try infinity. \par \par \uc2  BoJ also has significant legal reasons to challenge the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposal. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not its mandate, it is MoF's. The governing laws for the BoJ would have to be changed to include \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as one of its mandates. \par \par \uc2  US Treasury is far from warm to the policy as well as mentioned above. Deputy Treasury Secretary Dam is in Tokyo today to meet with Japanese officials on economic policy. At a press conference Tuesday before departing, he said \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not part of the planned discussions. He insinuated he was not going to bring it up and instead would focus on the pace of structural reform...how to speed it up. US Treasury can accept a weaker yen if it follows structural reform that weaken Japan's economic fundamentals in the short-term. But embracing a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy aimed at export-led growth and higher imported prices is unacceptable to the US. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  simply reflects desperation. BoJ has blocked adopting an inflation target, so officials have moved on to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, the Koizumi government has to make a far more convincing case that he is serious about bank reform. Recent bank earnings and loan write-offs are a start. But more needs to be done ASAP and fewer distractions in the policy debate like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen will help keep politicians and the market better focused. \par \par \uc2  -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com \par \par \uc2  (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) \par \par \uc2  1057 4166 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011205dxc500cnm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
