\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ board member backs buying foreign bonds. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2001\par 08:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 4 (Reuters) - A member of the Bank of Japan's policy board wants the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy 200 billion yen ($1.61 billion) a month of foreign bonds as part of its monetary easing, according to the minutes of the board's October 29 meeting. \par \par \uc2  The minutes, released on Tuesday, showed the member also said the BOJ should increase outright buying of Japanese government bonds to 800 billion yen a month from 600 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  The board member, who was not identified but was widely thought to be policy "dove" Nobuyuki Nakahara, said such regular purchases would not be taken as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under the ambit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  No vote was taken on the suggestion at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  But the discussion underlines the view that the BOJ is running out of policy options and needs to consider unprecedented steps to further ease monetary policy to halt a steady decline in prices and bolster the world's second largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Proponents say buying foreign bonds would help weaken the yen, boost exports and help fight deflation. \par \par \uc2  The problem, critics say, is that massive amounts of money would be needed to have any real impact on the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Also, many say, the BOJ would be overstepping its mandate if it was seen to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  However, Japanese Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka has said it would be worthwhile for the BOJ to consider buying foreign bonds as part of its policy \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's so-called "quantitative easing" policy - in which it floods the money market with liquidity by monitoring the excess funds banks are parking at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has so far had little effect in reviving the long-stagnant economy. \par \par \uc2  Because of this, the board member wanted a more aggressive liquidity supply, the minutes showed. \par \par \uc2  "(The member) added that it was appropriate to increase outright JGB buying to 800 billion yen per month and to regularly buy 200 billion yen of foreign bonds as a means to pursue quantitative easing," the minutes said. \par \par \uc2  Purchases of foreign bond purchases - generally understood to mean mainly U.S. Treasuries - would require dollar buying for yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as well as increased yen liquidity supply to the private-sector banking system. \par \par \uc2  This would effectively weaken the yen. A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would, in turn, help push up import prices and boost exports, thereby assisting the BOJ's efforts to halt deflation, which has plagued Japan for the past two years. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has insisted that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was in the hands of the government by law, and BOJ Governor Hayami is known to prefer a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  UNCHANGED SINCE SEPT 18 \par \par \uc2  The board voted 8-1 to leave monetary policy unchanged, keeping its quantitative easing target - the balance of banks' current account deposits at the BOJ - at "more than six trillion yen", the minutes showed. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ also left the largely symbolic official discount rate at 0.10 percent that day. \par \par \uc2  It was the second meeting in a row that the board voted to keep its keep policy unchanged, after easing on August 14 and September 18. \par \par \uc2  In reality, the BOJ has been providing much more liquidity than its stated target, but a majority of board members wanted to keep the target loose so as to cope with increasingly volatile fund demand, the minutes said. \par \par \uc2  The members noted that with interest rates at virtually zero, investors are not offering funds in the call market, the main marketplace for financial institutions to trade short-term money. \par \par \uc2  This had created patches of fund shortages even when the banking system as a whole was awash with free money, the minutes said. \par \par \uc2  "In view of such discussions, many members agreed that more time was needed to determine whether fund injection of more than six trillion yen would have more ripple effect on the market," the minutes said. \par \par \uc2  The sole dissenter to an unchanged policy stance was Nakahara. \par \par \uc2  Nakahara formally proposed raising the current account deposit target to around 10 trillion yen, dropping a limit on the amount of JGBs the BOJ can hold. \par \par \uc2  He also proposed the BOJ aim for a consumer price index level for January-March 2003 at or above the average in January-March 2001 - a so-called inflation-targeting policy, which most BOJ officials have been reluctant to adopt. \par \par \uc2  Nakahara's proposals were voted down 8-1, the minutes showed. ($1=124.07 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011204dxc400fg7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
