\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Welcomes Recent Yen Depreciation - FT \b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2001\par 00:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan welcomes the recent depreciation of the yen, even though it has no intention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Monday, the Financial Times reported in its Tuesday overseas edition. \par \par \uc2  "I think the yen has been too strong and the movement in the last couple of days hasn't been bad at all," the official said, according to the FT. He said further depreciation could be warranted by economic fundamentals, though "how much, and to what extent, is really difficult to say". \par \par \uc2  However, he said Japan wouldn't take up unofficial U.S. suggestions that it should buy U.S. Treasurys to weaken the yen and generate inflationary pressures at home. "As far as I am aware there has been no change in the U.S. exchange rate policy," he said, the FT reported. \par \par \uc2  He wouldn't speculate on why unnamed U.S. officials would comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - but admitted it has had desirable results, the FT reported. \par \par \uc2  The official said the idea of buying U.S. Treasurys to weaken the yen had been floated by "one or two members of the Bank of Japan policy board". It isn't supported by the government, which would have to authorize such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  "We don't want to be seen to be criticizing these Bank of Japan officials," the MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  "Certainly monetary policy should be further relaxed. But there are lots of domestic financial assets that the Bank could buy to increase liquidity." \par \par \uc2  It would be wrong for Japan to manipulate its exchange rate to export its deflation to other countries, he said. "Devaluing your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like raising tariffs. This is what happened in the 1930s. It isn't acceptable to the international community." \par \par \uc2  There is no appetite for an artificial adjustment of the exchange rate to the levels economists believe would be necessary to break Japan's deflation - perhaps Y160 or Y170 to the dollar, the FT said. This could cause panic. But the government is happy for the yen to depreciate further providing this is market-led and reflects fundamentals, the FT reported. \par \par \uc2  Other \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials on Monday speculated that the yen could slide to Y130 or Y140 without triggering a wave of competitive devaluations in Asia, the FT said. \par \par \uc2  The senior official said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as it had in September if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved out of line with fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  As the government is pleased with the current level of Y124 this could imply \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves abruptly up again, the FT said. Bank of Japan officials meanwhile suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't unanimous in its opposition to purchases of U.S. Treasurys, claiming that there was some sympathy for the proposal, the newspaper reported. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011126dxbq001b3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
