\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Japan faces limits to yen-weakening ambitions. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 963 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2001\par 09:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 19 (Reuters) - Japanese policymakers, running short of options to stop a slide into recession, are turning to the trusty old policy tool of a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a last resort. \par \par \uc2  But analysts say their room for manoeuvre is slimmer than in the past as any deliberate weakening will irritate neighbouring Asian countries struggling with their own economic downturns, leaving Japan little choice but to rely on market forces for now. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan (BOJ) and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  both favour a weaker yen," said Taisuke Tanaka, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  "I think they regard levels between 120 and 130 yen as neutral. If the yen becomes strong beyond that level, they will try to restrain it. And of course they will be even happier as long as the yen weakens," he added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 123.05/10 yen on Monday, from lows around 115.80 yen in late September. \par \par \uc2  Should the yen rise above 120 per dollar, analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling the yen, as it did aggressively in September, when the dollar fell in the aftermath of the attacks on U.S. targets. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen benefits Japan's economy by making its flagging exports cheaper on world markets, and should also help cure the scourge of deflation by raising the price of foreign imports. \par \par \uc2  "Weakening the yen is by no means a panacea. But it does have positive effects on the economy," said Tanaka. "It is vital for Japan not to have a strong yen when the economy is weak." \par \par \uc2  It seems especially attractive to policymakers as other options for boosting the economy are fast running out. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates are at virtually zero after a series of cuts by the BOJ, which is also flooding the money market with trillions of yen in funds to stimulate the economy. \par \par \uc2  On the fiscal side, the days of massive public stimulus spending, which helped Japan amass the biggest public debt of any industrialised country, seem to be drawing to a close as premier Junichiro Koizumi tries to advance painful reforms. \par \par \uc2  Japan's public debt of 666 trillion yen ($5.4 trillion), amounts to a hefty 128 percent of Japan's gross domestic product, raising calls among economists that the government has to work to curtail its debt levels or face bankruptcy. \par \par \uc2  Even with this heavy public spending and ultra-loose monetary policy, the economy may already be in its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  The government is forecasting negative growth for this business year, while the BOJ on Monday downgraded its view of the economy for the sixth straight month, citing weaker consumption. \par \par \uc2  Recognising this, Finance Ministry officials have recently been lining up to say that a strengthening of the yen would be unjustifiable. \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, a top financial diplomat for the ministry, said last Wednesday that any speculative rise in the yen was undesirable, arguing that the U.S. economy still had fundamental strength. \par \par \uc2  "It is not favourable if the yen moves in a speculative manner. Our stance remains unchanged that we would quickly prevent such a move," Mizoguchi said. \par \par \uc2  A STEALTHY WEAKENING \par \par \uc2  Some analysts are expecting Japan to deliberately weaken the yen by buying the dollar, or dollar-denominated assets such as U.S. Treasuries, for the yen. \par \par \uc2  But a majority think the authorities are likely to take a less aggressive stance, for fear of angering other countries while the global economy is still tottering in the wake of September 11. \par \par \uc2  "It will be difficult to say the yen is extremely high in trade-weighted terms right now. Japanese exporters are generally happy with the dollar between 120 and 130 yen," said Susumu Okano, chief economist at Daiwa Institute of Research. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that with Japan clearly not the only economy in trouble, a more subtle approach would have to be taken whereby the yen's current level in the low 120s is perhaps seen as paving the way for a longer-term period of yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities will not seek to weaken the yen intentionally, although it's clear they do not accept a stronger yen beyond 120 yen," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  And after struggling to restrain the yen in the days after Setember 11, market forces may have turned in Tokyo's favour. Many economists now expect the U.S. economy to rebound relatively quickly, helped by a series of deep rate cuts, while Japan's economy looks as terminally sick as ever. \par \par \uc2  CHINA RISING, YUAN NOT \par \par \uc2  But the euro, which fell to a six-week low around 106.90 yen recently, and increasingly the Chinese yuan, are more of a worry. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts said Tokyo is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the euro aggressively, because of its limited role in Japanese trade. \par \par \uc2  With China's trade clout set to grow after its recent accession to the World Trade Organisation, Tokyo is already paying more attention to the yuan's value. \par \par \uc2  Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said last week that many people think the yuan may be undervalued and some economists say that its purchasing power suggests a level of about 7.0 per dollar, compared to its current peg of 8.28. \par \par \uc2  But here too, Tokyo is unlikely to apply direct pressure given already prickly trade relations with its giant neighbour, likely prefering instead to wait for China's authorities to act. \par \par \uc2  "China now has a good deal of foreign reserves, which will enable it to deal with a sudden fall in the yuan. I think China is thinking of adopting a floating system in the medium-term," Okano of Daiwa said. ($1=122.86 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011119dxbj00h4u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
