\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-ECB's Noyer says euro has room to appreciate. \b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2001\par 10:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 15 (Reuters) - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) Vice President Christian Noyer said on Thursday there was room for the euro, which fell near a three-month low this week despite last week's credit easing by the ECB, to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB is interested in a strong euro based on strong economic fundamentals," Noyer told a news conference, adding that there were no major macroeconomic imbalances in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  "Because of this absence of major imbalances, we firmly believe there is room for appreciation and the present exchange rate does not reflect the inner strength of the euro, and that sooner or later the external value will be higher again." \par \par \uc2  The ECB cut key short-term interest rates by half a percentage point last week in response to falling inflation and a darkening economic outlook. Noyer was in Tokyo attending a financial symposium ahead of the introduction of euro notes and coins on January 1, 2002. \par \par \uc2  As of 0826 GMT, the euro was trading at $0.8822 after dipping to below $0.88 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Earlier at the symposium, Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, said the euro was undervalued given the fundamentals of the euro-zone economy. \par \par \uc2  "Taking into consideration economic fundamentals, the euro has been undervalued since its establishment," said Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  He added that appropriate macroeconomic policy and structural reforms were needed for a recovery in European economies, and that they would make the euro even more attractive as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked whether Japan would boost the volume of euros in its external reserves, Kuroda said that would be difficult since changing the composition of reserves could be akin to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "G7 nations are always in contact about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. "To change the composition of foreign reserves would mean \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it cannot be easily done." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda added that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep a balanced mix of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its reserves. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the ECB would focus more on weakening demand than M3, which the ECB uses as a key reference for money supply, Noyer said the current rise in M3 was misleading. \par \par \uc2  "Loans to the private sector are declining, so there is a difference between money and credit, which shows that something is happening that needs to be interpreted," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Our conclusion is, for the moment M3 is not telling anything." \par \par \uc2  Noyer said that was why the ECB had focused on information from economic indicators in its last policy decision. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011115dxbf00ij3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
